onConfidence:["Low"], pmid:"23231993", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23231993"}}, {start: {_id:1531}, end: {_id:432}, properties:{evidence:"Molecules related to cytoskeleton maintenance, calcium metabolism and cellular survival such as glial fibrillary acidic protein, vimentin, tropomyosin, collapsin response mediator protein-2, calmodulin, S100-P, annexin A1, alpha-internexin, alpha- and beta-synuclein, alpha-B-crystalline, fascin-1, ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal esterase and thymosine were altered between AD and NDC pools. Our experiments suggest that WM activities become globally impaired during the course of AD with significant morphological, biochemical and functional consequential implications for gray matter function and cognitive deficits.", annotationSubgraph:["Cytokine signaling subgraph", "Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Low"], pmid:"23231993", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23231993"}}, {start: {_id:1532}, end: {_id:432}, properties:{evidence:"Molecules related to cytoskeleton maintenance, calcium metabolism and cellular survival such as glial fibrillary acidic protein, vimentin, tropomyosin, collapsin response mediator protein-2, calmodulin, S100-P, annexin A1, alpha-internexin, alpha- and beta-synuclein, alpha-B-crystalline, fascin-1, ubiquitin carboxyl-terminal esterase and thymosine were altered between AD and NDC pools. Our experiments suggest that WM activities become globally impaired during the course of AD with significant morphological, biochemical and functional consequential implications for gray matter function and cognitive deficits.", annotationSubgraph:["Cytokine signaling subgraph", "Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Low"], pmid:"23231993", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23231993"}}, {start: {_id:666}, end: {_id:43}, properties:{evidence:"We also found that human islet amylin and the prion protein fragment (PrP106-126), peptides that acquire beta-pleated sheet conformation in water solutions and have been reported to form ion channels across planar bilayer membranes, also increase cytosolic free calcium in GT1-7 neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Amylin subgraph"], pmid:"10799482", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10799482"}}, {start: {_id:1595}, end: {_id:1787}, properties:{evidence:"Rivastigmine significantly increased c-Fos immunoreactivity in medial prefrontal cortex and the hippocampus, but not in the septum and dorsal raphe nucleus.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Hippocampus", "Prefrontal Cortex", "Raphe Nuclei"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"16426583", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16426583"}}, {start: {_id:1595}, end: {_id:1962}, properties:{evidence:"5-HT depletion decreased ACh-induced c-Fos immunoreactivity in the dentate gyrus.", annotationSubgraph:["Serotonergic subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Dentate Gyrus"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"16426583", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16426583"}}, {start: {_id:1999}, end: {_id:76}, properties:{evidence:"We have shown with cultured cerebral cortical normal (i.e., untransformed) adult human astrocytes (NAHAs) that exogenous amyloid- beta peptides (Abetas) stimulate the astrocytes to make and secrete large amounts of Abetas and nitric oxide by a mechanism mediated through the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR).", annotationSubgraph:["Nitric oxide subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebral Cortex", "Astrocytes"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24948534", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24948534"}}, {start: {_id:2035}, end: {_id:1225}, properties:{evidence:"High-fat load and glucose alone produced an increase of nitrotyrosine, ICAM-1, VCAM-1, and E-selectin plasma levels in normal and diabetic subjects.", annotationSubgraph:["Cell adhesion subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Plasma"], pmid:"14988255", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"14988255"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:2048}, properties:{evidence:"Furthermore, recent studies have demonstrated that age-related androgen depletion results in accumulation of beta-amyloid protein and thereby acts as a risk factor for the development of Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Androgen subgraph"], pmid:"24177288", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24177288"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:2170}, end: {_id:2171}, properties:{evidence:"In studies of dementias and Alzheimer's disease (AD), some studies have shown that antihypertensive drugs, including angiotensin- converting enzyme inhibitors, have some moderate effects on cognitive decline, but that the angiotensin receptor antagonist losartan has a significantly beneficial effect.", annotationSubgraph:["Renin-angiotensin subgraph"], pmid:"14996614", annotationDisease:["dementia", "Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"14996614"}}, {start: {_id:2083}, end: {_id:93}, properties:{evidence:"However, the possible role of MPO and enzymatically inactive MPO (iMPO) as the choreographers of the destruction done by TNF-alpha and ROS is not generally recognized.", annotationSubgraph:["Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph", "Myeloperoxidase subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18554520", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18554520"}}, {start: {_id:2015}, end: {_id:1760}, properties:{evidence:"Our preliminary data suggest that the CYP2D6 polymorphism influences both donepezil metabolism and therapeutic outcome and that a knowledge of a patient's CYP2D6 genotype together with donepezil concentration measurements might be useful in the context of improving the clinical efficacy of donepezil therapy.", annotationSubgraph:["Paroxetine subgraph"], pmid:"16845507", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16845507"}}, {start: {_id:2367}, end: {_id:2368}, properties:{evidence:"Nanomedicine: action of metal nanoparticles on neuronal nitric oxide synthase-fluorimetric analysis on the mechanism for fibrillogenesis.", annotationSubgraph:["Nitric oxide subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Low"], pmid:"24293250", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24293250"}}, {start: {_id:2431}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, 5-LO targeted gene disruption or its in vivo selective pharmacological inhibition results in a significant reduction of Abeta, CREB and gamma-secretase levels. These data establish a novel functional role for 5-LO in regulating endogenous formation of Abeta levels in the central nervous system.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "Eicosanoids signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Central Nervous System"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21280074", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21280074"}}, {start: {_id:2508}, end: {_id:93}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, the scavengers of reactive oxygen species (ROS) including Trolox and N-acetylcysteine (NAC) abrogated the SOD1(G93A)-induced increase in ASK1 activity (Supplementary Figure 2), suggesting that SOD1(G93A) induces ASK1 activation in a ROS-dependent manner.", annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Neurons"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"25018698", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25018698"}}, {start: {_id:174}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, apoE-positive newly formed plaques were seen more frequently in APOE epsilon4/4 cases than in non-APOE epsilon4/4 individuals, thereby underlining the potentially crucial role of apoE for the development of Abeta deposits.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"16195918", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16195918"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:2722}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, our recent studies further demonstrated that (1) apoE mediates sulfatide depletion in amyloid-beta precursor protein transgenic mice; (2) sulfatides enhance amyloid beta (Abeta) peptides binding to apoE-associated particles; (3) Abeta42 content notably correlates with sulfatide content in CSF;(4) sulfatides markedly enhance the uptake of Abeta peptides; and (5) abnormal sulfatide- facilitated Abeta uptake results in the accumulation of Abeta in lysosomes.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20052565", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20052565"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:2722}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, our recent studies further demonstrated that (1) apoE mediates sulfatide depletion in amyloid-beta precursor protein transgenic mice; (2) sulfatides enhance amyloid beta (Abeta) peptides binding to apoE-associated particles; (3) Abeta42 content notably correlates with sulfatide content in CSF;(4) sulfatides markedly enhance the uptake of Abeta peptides; and (5) abnormal sulfatide- facilitated Abeta uptake results in the accumulation of Abeta in lysosomes.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20052565", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20052565"}}, {start: {_id:1691}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Leptin, an adipocytokine involved in cell survival and in learning, has been demonstrated to regulate Abeta production and tau hyperphosphorylation in transgenic mice for AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20157255", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20157255"}}, {start: {_id:376}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Increasing evidence suggests that the low density lipoprotein receptor-related protein ( LRP ) affects the processing of amyloid precursor protein ( APP ) and amyloid beta ( Abeta ) protein production as well as mediates the clearance of Abeta from the brain.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Low density lipoprotein subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"15772078", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15772078"}}, {start: {_id:36}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Nicastrin also binds carboxy-terminal derivatives of beta-amyloid precursor protein ( betaAPP ) , and pmodulates the production of the amyloid beta-peptide ( A beta ) from these derivatives.", annotationSubgraph:["Gamma secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"10993067", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10993067"}}, {start: {_id:36}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Increasing evidences have shown that nicastrin (NCSTN) plays a crucial role in gamma-cleavage of the amyloid precursor protein (APP).", annotationSubgraph:["Gamma secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"16423463", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16423463"}}, {start: {_id:1252}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Our observations indicate that Bim is a proapoptotic effector of Abeta and of dysregulated cell cycle proteins in AD and identify both Bim and cell cycle elements as potential therapeutic targets.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17251431", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17251431"}}, {start: {_id:1345}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"A possible cell surface target for Abetas is the p75 neurotrophin receptor (p75(NTR)).", annotationSubgraph:["Nerve growth factor subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17385278", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17385278"}}, {start: {_id:726}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"SorLA has been shown to be down regulated in Alzheimer's disease brains, interact with ApoE, and pmodulate Abeta production", annotationSubgraph:["Low density lipoprotein subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"16930450", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16930450"}}, {start: {_id:3695}, end: {_id:432}, properties:{evidence:"Insulin like growth factor-I (IGF-I) and insulin signaling: The underlying mechanism of neuronal survival with ABeta¸ is emerging. Neuronal synapses and astrocytes of memory-processing brain regions possess insulin receptors (IRs) [99] which when activated by insulin facilitate synaptic plasticity in normal brain [100]. IR and Insulin- like growth factor I (IGF-I) receptors consist of a-subunits and transmembrane ÃƒÅ¸-subunits. Binding of insulin or IGF-I to the a-subunit increases the intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, and causes autophosphorylation of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, thus triggering tyrosine phosphorylation of insulin receptor substrate (IRS)-1 and IRS-2, as well as Shc [101] as an important pathway of cell survival. To protect against ABeta¸ toxicity, the tyrosine- phosphorylated sites create binding sites for various signal- transducing molecules containing Src homology-2 domain, such as phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) and growth factor receptor-bound protein 2 (Grb2), thus activating PI3K/phosphoinostide-dependent kinase 1 (PDK1)/Akt (protein kinase B)/glycogen synthase kinase (GSK)-3a/-3ÃƒÅ¸ and Ras/Raf-1/mitogen-activated protein kinase/extracellular-signal regulated kinase (MEK/ERK) signaling pathways [101]. In normal brain, IGF-I and insulin promote glucose utilization, energy metabolism, and neuronal survival [102], largely through PI3K/Akt/GSK-3ÃƒÅ¸ signaling [103, 104].Consistent with positive effects of insulin on synaptic plasticity [105], acute insulin treatment improved memory in rats [106] and also in normaladults and AD patients [107] by strongly activating ERK and Akt and blocking c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) activation in a PI3K-dependent manner [108]. The mechanism involves many steps beginning with ABeta¸ activation of IGF-1/insulin receptors by locally produced IGF-1 or, possibly, ABeta¸ monomers may bind to IGF-1/insulin receptors, as already shown for ABeta¸ oligomers", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:3696}, end: {_id:432}, properties:{evidence:"Insulin like growth factor-I (IGF-I) and insulin signaling: The underlying mechanism of neuronal survival with ABeta¸ is emerging. Neuronal synapses and astrocytes of memory-processing brain regions possess insulin receptors (IRs) [99] which when activated by insulin facilitate synaptic plasticity in normal brain [100]. IR and Insulin- like growth factor I (IGF-I) receptors consist of a-subunits and transmembrane ÃƒÅ¸-subunits. Binding of insulin or IGF-I to the a-subunit increases the intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, and causes autophosphorylation of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, thus triggering tyrosine phosphorylation of insulin receptor substrate (IRS)-1 and IRS-2, as well as Shc [101] as an important pathway of cell survival. To protect against ABeta¸ toxicity, the tyrosine- phosphorylated sites create binding sites for various signal- transducing molecules containing Src homology-2 domain, such as phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) and growth factor receptor-bound protein 2 (Grb2), thus activating PI3K/phosphoinostide-dependent kinase 1 (PDK1)/Akt (protein kinase B)/glycogen synthase kinase (GSK)-3a/-3ÃƒÅ¸ and Ras/Raf-1/mitogen-activated protein kinase/extracellular-signal regulated kinase (MEK/ERK) signaling pathways [101]. In normal brain, IGF-I and insulin promote glucose utilization, energy metabolism, and neuronal survival [102], largely through PI3K/Akt/GSK-3ÃƒÅ¸ signaling [103, 104].Consistent with positive effects of insulin on synaptic plasticity [105], acute insulin treatment improved memory in rats [106] and also in normaladults and AD patients [107] by strongly activating ERK and Akt and blocking c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) activation in a PI3K-dependent manner [108]. The mechanism involves many steps beginning with ABeta¸ activation of IGF-1/insulin receptors by locally produced IGF-1 or, possibly, ABeta¸ monomers may bind to IGF-1/insulin receptors, as already shown for ABeta¸ oligomers", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:3697}, end: {_id:432}, properties:{evidence:"Insulin like growth factor-I (IGF-I) and insulin signaling: The underlying mechanism of neuronal survival with ABeta¸ is emerging. Neuronal synapses and astrocytes of memory-processing brain regions possess insulin receptors (IRs) [99] which when activated by insulin facilitate synaptic plasticity in normal brain [100]. IR and Insulin- like growth factor I (IGF-I) receptors consist of a-subunits and transmembrane ÃƒÅ¸-subunits. Binding of insulin or IGF-I to the a-subunit increases the intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, and causes autophosphorylation of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, thus triggering tyrosine phosphorylation of insulin receptor substrate (IRS)-1 and IRS-2, as well as Shc [101] as an important pathway of cell survival. To protect against ABeta¸ toxicity, the tyrosine- phosphorylated sites create binding sites for various signal- transducing molecules containing Src homology-2 domain, such as phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) and growth factor receptor-bound protein 2 (Grb2), thus activating PI3K/phosphoinostide-dependent kinase 1 (PDK1)/Akt (protein kinase B)/glycogen synthase kinase (GSK)-3a/-3ÃƒÅ¸ and Ras/Raf-1/mitogen-activated protein kinase/extracellular-signal regulated kinase (MEK/ERK) signaling pathways [101]. In normal brain, IGF-I and insulin promote glucose utilization, energy metabolism, and neuronal survival [102], largely through PI3K/Akt/GSK-3ÃƒÅ¸ signaling [103, 104].Consistent with positive effects of insulin on synaptic plasticity [105], acute insulin treatment improved memory in rats [106] and also in normaladults and AD patients [107] by strongly activating ERK and Akt and blocking c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) activation in a PI3K-dependent manner [108]. The mechanism involves many steps beginning with ABeta¸ activation of IGF-1/insulin receptors by locally produced IGF-1 or, possibly, ABeta¸ monomers may bind to IGF-1/insulin receptors, as already shown for ABeta¸ oligomers", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:3698}, end: {_id:432}, properties:{evidence:"Insulin like growth factor-I (IGF-I) and insulin signaling: The underlying mechanism of neuronal survival with ABeta¸ is emerging. Neuronal synapses and astrocytes of memory-processing brain regions possess insulin receptors (IRs) [99] which when activated by insulin facilitate synaptic plasticity in normal brain [100]. IR and Insulin- like growth factor I (IGF-I) receptors consist of a-subunits and transmembrane ÃƒÅ¸-subunits. Binding of insulin or IGF-I to the a-subunit increases the intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, and causes autophosphorylation of the ÃƒÅ¸-subunit, thus triggering tyrosine phosphorylation of insulin receptor substrate (IRS)-1 and IRS-2, as well as Shc [101] as an important pathway of cell survival. To protect against ABeta¸ toxicity, the tyrosine- phosphorylated sites create binding sites for various signal- transducing molecules containing Src homology-2 domain, such as phosphoinositide 3-kinase (PI3K) and growth factor receptor-bound protein 2 (Grb2), thus activating PI3K/phosphoinostide-dependent kinase 1 (PDK1)/Akt (protein kinase B)/glycogen synthase kinase (GSK)-3a/-3ÃƒÅ¸ and Ras/Raf-1/mitogen-activated protein kinase/extracellular-signal regulated kinase (MEK/ERK) signaling pathways [101]. In normal brain, IGF-I and insulin promote glucose utilization, energy metabolism, and neuronal survival [102], largely through PI3K/Akt/GSK-3ÃƒÅ¸ signaling [103, 104].Consistent with positive effects of insulin on synaptic plasticity [105], acute insulin treatment improved memory in rats [106] and also in normaladults and AD patients [107] by strongly activating ERK and Akt and blocking c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK) activation in a PI3K-dependent manner [108]. The mechanism involves many steps beginning with ABeta¸ activation of IGF-1/insulin receptors by locally produced IGF-1 or, possibly, ABeta¸ monomers may bind to IGF-1/insulin receptors, as already shown for ABeta¸ oligomers", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}] AS row
