ARE pathway for treatment of AD and suggest that the Nrf2-ARE pathway may represent a potential therapeutic strategy to pursue in AD in humans, particularly in view of the multiple mechanisms by which Nrf2 can exert its protective effects.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"19805328", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19805328"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2184}, properties:{evidence:"Nrf2-encoding NFE2L2 haplotypes influence disease progression but not risk in Alzheimer's disease and age-related cataract.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "Free radical formation subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Cataract", "Alzheimer Disease"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20064547", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20064547"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2184}, properties:{evidence:"However, one haplotype allele of NFE2L2 was associated with 2 years earlier age at AD onset (p(c)=0.013)", annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20064547", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20064547"}}, {start: {_id:1148}, end: {_id:96}, properties:{evidence:"Associated with these inflammatory responses are tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha) and reactive oxygen species (ROS), both believed to be derived from brain microglia.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Microglia"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18554520", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18554520"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2083}, properties:{evidence:"In females, we found a significant association between MPO genotype and AD (P=0.034),", annotationSubgraph:["Myeloperoxidase subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"12946561", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12946561"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2205}, properties:{evidence:"In conclusion, the G-463A polymorphism of MPO was statistically associated with AD in a gender-specific manner. However, given the low significance of P value we suggest no causal effect of the MPO gene in AD, as also evidenced in a recent meta-analysis.", annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"12946561", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12946561"}}, {start: {_id:966}, end: {_id:1996}, properties:{evidence:"Our findings support the hypothesis that MMPs may influence the risk of dementia.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"16822591", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16822591"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2213}, properties:{evidence:"Herein, we conducted a meta-analysis to clarify the association between ESR1 polymorphisms and the occurrence of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Estrogen subgraph"], pmid:"24857745", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24857745"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2214}, properties:{evidence:"Tissue plasminogen activator arrests Alzheimer's disease pathogenesis.", annotationSubgraph:["Plasminogen activator subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24126163", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24126163"}}, {start: {_id:2217}, end: {_id:2214}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, we assessed changes in endogenous net tPA activity in WT mice following morphine administration, epileptic seizures, traumatic brain injury and ischaemic stroke-neurological settings in which tPA has a known functional role.", annotationSubgraph:["Plasminogen activator subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Epilepsy", "Brain Injuries, Traumatic", "Stroke"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], pmid:"21519332", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21519332"}}] AS row
