UNWIND [{start: {_id:2469}, end: {_id:1141}, properties:{evidence:"Glimepiride released CD14 from RAW 264 cells and microglial cells. Pre-treatment with glimepiride significantly reduced TNF, IL-1 and IL-6 secretion from RAW 264 and microglial cells incubated with LPS, Abeta42, alphaSN and PrP82-146. More recently, the concentrations of soluble CD14 were found to be elevated in AD and PD patients.", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24952384", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24952384"}}, {start: {_id:2469}, end: {_id:746}, properties:{evidence:"Glimepiride released CD14 from RAW 264 cells and microglial cells. Pre-treatment with glimepiride significantly reduced TNF, IL-1 and IL-6 secretion from RAW 264 and microglial cells incubated with LPS, Abeta42, alphaSN and PrP82-146. More recently, the concentrations of soluble CD14 were found to be elevated in AD and PD patients.", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], pmid:"24952384", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24952384"}}, {start: {_id:2475}, end: {_id:96}, properties:{evidence:"In support of this mechanism, in vitro, rapamycin significantly inhibits the production of NO, TNF-Î± in BV2 microglials by modulating NF-ÎºB signaling.", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph", "mTOR signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24923557", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24923557"}}, {start: {_id:2540}, end: {_id:2061}, properties:{evidence:"As a step in searching for possible antioxidative mechanisms of riluzole, we tested in test-tube conditions its effects on the activity of PLA2, an enzyme that is linked to oxidative injury via the AA cascades (Janssen-Timmen et al., 1994 ; Katsuki and Okuda, 1995). Riluzole (10-100 Î¼M), in a concentration-dependent manner, attenuated the activity of cPLA2, but not that of group I and group II PLA2", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph"], pmid:"9930745", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"9930745"}}, {start: {_id:2540}, end: {_id:2557}, properties:{evidence:"In our study, following CLP plasma TNF-Î± levels were significantly increased, while these increases were partially depressed by riluzole treatment. Recently, 2 studies have shown that glutamate induces TNF-Î± release from neuronal cells in pathological conditions, and this process may be involved in apoptotic neuronal cell death. Moreover, they confirmed that NMDA antagonists do not affect TNF-Î± level under basal conditions [38].", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18718604", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18718604"}}, {start: {_id:2540}, end: {_id:1940}, properties:{evidence:"Riluzole has been shown to inhibit vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)-stimulated protein kinase c (PKC), beta2 activation and cell proliferation in bovine retinal endothelial cell and human umbilical vein endothelial cell cultures (17).", annotationSubgraph:["Vascular endothelial growth factor subgraph"], pmid:"19528481", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19528481"}}, {start: {_id:2540}, end: {_id:1940}, properties:{evidence:"Of the signaling molecules downstream of VEGF receptors, PKC has been demonstrated to be critical in mediating endothelial cell proliferation.17 18 19 Although inhibition of PKC may seem a reasonable approach to curtail endothelial cell proliferation in proliferative retinopathies,20 21 PKC serves diverse essential normal roles in intracellular signaling, indicating broad-spectrum inhibition of all PKCs may have harmful side effects, including cell apoptotic process. Riluzole inhibited PKC activity in cortical cell cultures, and also inhibited the activity of purified PKC in vitro.", annotationSubgraph:["Vascular endothelial growth factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"16303979", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16303979"}}, {start: {_id:2540}, end: {_id:1949}, properties:{evidence:"Riluzole also prevented or attenuated ischemia-induced retinal cell death (necrosis and apoptotic process) and reduced the activation of p-JNK, c-jun phosphorylation, and the increase of cytoskeletal proteins induced by ischemic injury. The present study provides the first demonstration that retinal ischemia evokes a lasting activation of p-JNK and phosphorylation of c-jun at serine 73- Riluzole totally blocked the ischemia-induced increase in p-JNK expression during postischemia reperfusion.", annotationSubgraph:["MAPK-JNK subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"10067977", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10067977"}}, {start: {_id:2540}, end: {_id:567}, properties:{evidence:"The levels of the anti-apoptotic proteins Bcl-2 and HSP70 were higher in the riluzole groups than in the control. Furthermore, co- administration of riluzole with morphine significantly decreased caspase-3 protein levels and glutamate content of the cerebral cortex compared with the control. In conclusion, we found that icv administration of riluzole attenuates morphine-induced apoptosis in the cerebral cortex after the development of morphine tolerance.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21857080", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21857080"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:205}, properties:{evidence:"In conclusion, inhibition of both BuChE and AChE with rivastigmine was improved the cognition without affecting on the peripheral IR in the elderly patients with LOAD by HOMA.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22323348", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22323348"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:272}, properties:{evidence:"In conclusion, inhibition of both BuChE and AChE with rivastigmine was improved the cognition without affecting on the peripheral IR in the elderly patients with LOAD by HOMA.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22323348", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22323348"}}, {start: {_id:2564}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"Furthermore, Meserine (7.5 mg/kg) injected intraperitoneally once daily for 3 weeks lowered APP level by 28% and Abeta42 level by 42% in APP/PS1 transgenic mouse cerebrum. And both APP and Abeta42 lowering action of Meserine maintained longer than that of rivastigmin", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebrum"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24279603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24279603"}}, {start: {_id:2564}, end: {_id:22}, properties:{evidence:"Furthermore, Meserine (7.5 mg/kg) injected intraperitoneally once daily for 3 weeks lowered APP level by 28% and Abeta42 level by 42% in APP/PS1 transgenic mouse cerebrum. And both APP and Abeta42 lowering action of Meserine maintained longer than that of rivastigmin", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebrum"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24279603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24279603"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:96}, properties:{evidence:"Rivastigmine decreased the reactivity of encephalitogenic T-cells and the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-Î±, IFN-gamma and IL-17) without affecting IL-10 production.", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18692909", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18692909"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:224}, properties:{evidence:"Rivastigmine decreased the reactivity of encephalitogenic T-cells and the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-Î±, IFN-gamma and IL-17) without affecting IL-10 production.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18692909", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18692909"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:2045}, properties:{evidence:"Rivastigmine decreased the reactivity of encephalitogenic T-cells and the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-Î±, IFN-gamma and IL-17) without affecting IL-10 production.", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18692909", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18692909"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:205}, properties:{evidence:"Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase activity by rivastigmine decreases lipopolysaccharide-induced IL-1beta expression in the hypothalamus of ewes", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"23291013", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23291013"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:97}, properties:{evidence:"Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase activity by rivastigmine decreases lipopolysaccharide-induced IL-1beta expression in the hypothalamus of ewes", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"23291013", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23291013"}}, {start: {_id:1787}, end: {_id:97}, properties:{evidence:"Rivastigmine treatment decreased (P â‰¤ 0.01) LPS-induced elevation of IL-1beta and IL-1R1 mRNA expression in these structures ( Fig. 1). In contrast, we did not find any effect of immune stress or rivastigmine treatment on CHRNA7 gene expression in the hypothalamus. which showed that rivastigmine suppresses both the AChE activity and synthesis of IL-1beta in the hypothalamus", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Hypothalamus"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"23291013", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23291013"}}, {start: {_id:2579}, end: {_id:97}, properties:{evidence:"The moderating effect of rivastigmine on the endotoxin-induced suppression of GnRH/LH secretion may result from the inhibition of pro-inflammatory cytokines released through the cholinergic anti- inflammatory pathway. AChE inhibitors lead to an increase in the concentration of ACh and activate the cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway (Borovikova et al., 2000). These inhibitors attenuate the cytokine release, including that of IL-1beta, IL-6 and TNFÎ±, which have been previously described both in vitro and in vivo ( Borovikova et al., 2000 and Pollak et al., 2005). The ability of rivastigmine to reduce the inflammatory action within the brain could have a profound effect on GnRH secretion, as numerous studies have reported that centrally acting pro-inflammatory cytokines, especially IL-1beta but also IL-1Î± and TNFÎ±, may be primarily responsible for the inhibition of GnRH/LH secretion", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"23557940", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23557940"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:DECREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:2579}, end: {_id:746}, properties:{evidence:"The moderating effect of rivastigmine on the endotoxin-induced suppression of GnRH/LH secretion may result from the inhibition of pro-inflammatory cytokines released through the cholinergic anti- inflammatory pathway. AChE inhibitors lead to an increase in the concentration of ACh and activate the cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway (Borovikova et al., 2000). These inhibitors attenuate the cytokine release, including that of IL-1beta, IL-6 and TNFÎ±, which have been previously described both in vitro and in vivo ( Borovikova et al., 2000 and Pollak et al., 2005). The ability of rivastigmine to reduce the inflammatory action within the brain could have a profound effect on GnRH secretion, as numerous studies have reported that centrally acting pro-inflammatory cytokines, especially IL-1beta but also IL-1Î± and TNFÎ±, may be primarily responsible for the inhibition of GnRH/LH secretion", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"23557940", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23557940"}}, {start: {_id:2579}, end: {_id:96}, properties:{evidence:"The moderating effect of rivastigmine on the endotoxin-induced suppression of GnRH/LH secretion may result from the inhibition of pro-inflammatory cytokines released through the cholinergic anti- inflammatory pathway. AChE inhibitors lead to an increase in the concentration of ACh and activate the cholinergic anti-inflammatory pathway (Borovikova et al., 2000). These inhibitors attenuate the cytokine release, including that of IL-1beta, IL-6 and TNFÎ±, which have been previously described both in vitro and in vivo ( Borovikova et al., 2000 and Pollak et al., 2005). The ability of rivastigmine to reduce the inflammatory action within the brain could have a profound effect on GnRH secretion, as numerous studies have reported that centrally acting pro-inflammatory cytokines, especially IL-1beta but also IL-1Î± and TNFÎ±, may be primarily responsible for the inhibition of GnRH/LH secretion", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"23557940", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23557940"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:1681}, properties:{evidence:"Compared with the control group, acidic buffer or plus cyclosporine A post-conditioning significantly improved myocardial performance, decreased cytochrome C release into the cytosol, increased Bcl-2 expression and decreased Bax expression, decreased sensitivity of mPTP-opening to [Ca2+] and the rate of apoptosis after reperfusion. These findings suggested that acidic buffer or plus cyclosporine A post- conditioning prevented apoptotic process-related mitochondrial permeabilization and provided the myocardial protection after cardioplegic arrest.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21490080", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21490080"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:1678}, properties:{evidence:"Compared with the control group, acidic buffer or plus cyclosporine A post-conditioning significantly improved myocardial performance, decreased cytochrome C release into the cytosol, increased Bcl-2 expression and decreased Bax expression, decreased sensitivity of mPTP-opening to [Ca2+] and the rate of apoptosis after reperfusion. These findings suggested that acidic buffer or plus cyclosporine A post- conditioning prevented apoptotic process-related mitochondrial permeabilization and provided the myocardial protection after cardioplegic arrest.", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21490080", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21490080"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:567}, properties:{evidence:"Pretreatment with cyclosporin A (500 nM, 30 h) significantly decreased caspase-3 activation during extended incubations with paraoxon, parathion, and TPPi (p < 0.05).", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"11032765", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11032765"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:567}, properties:{evidence:"Recent evidence indicates that reperfusion of the heart after a period of ischemia leads to the opening of the mitochondrial permeability transition pore (MPTP). The aim of this study was to investigate cardioprotective effects of cyclosporine A (CsA), an inhibitor of the MPTP, in an in vivo model of myocardial ischemia and reperfusionCsA significantly reduced infarct size (48.8 +/- 5.8% of left ventricle in vehicle + I/R group and 30.3 +/- 2.7% of left ventricle in CsA + I/R, respectively) and decreased caspase-3 activity in the myocardium [(0.62 +/- 0.17)/microg of protein and (0.42 +/- 0.15)/microg of protein, respectively] and relieved the injury of mitochondria. The cardioprotective effects of CsA might be associated with the protection of mitochondria and the inhibition of caspase-3 activity. It also suggests that the MPTP might play an important role in cardiomyocytes death after ischemia-reperfusion injury.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"17578461", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17578461"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:1049}, properties:{evidence:"In YAC46 MSNs, NMDA stimulated significantly higher activation of caspase-3 and caspase-9 but not caspase-8, and NMDA-induced caspase-3 and -9 activation was markedly attenuated by cyclosporin A", annotationSubgraph:["NMDA receptor", "Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"15033175", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15033175"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:1049}, properties:{evidence:"Cyclosporin A, an inhibitor of the mitochondrial permeability pore, partially decreased mitochondrial depolarization, caspase 3 activation, and caspase 9 activation, suggesting a role for mitochondrial dysfunction in these events.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"12857937", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12857937"}}, {start: {_id:2590}, end: {_id:973}, properties:{evidence:"Crystal structure of human cyclophilin D in complex with its inhibitor, cyclosporin A at 0.96-A resolution.", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial translocation subgraph"], pmid:"18076075", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18076075"}}, {start: {_id:2678}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, NPD1 suppresses Abeta42 peptide shedding by down-regulating beta-secretase-1 (BACE1) while activating the beta-secretase ADAM10 and up-regulating sAPPalpha, thus shifting the cleavage of betaAPP holoenzyme from an amyloidogenic into the non-amyloidogenic pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Beta secretase subgraph", "Neuroprotection subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21246057", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21246057"}}, {start: {_id:2678}, end: {_id:765}, properties:{evidence:"We also show that NPD1 downregulates Abeta42-triggered expression of the pro-inflammatory enzyme cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) and of B-94 (a TNF-alpha-inducible pro-inflammatory element) and apoptosis in HNG cells. Moreover, NPD1 suppresses Abeta42 peptide shedding by down- regulating beta-secretase-1 (BACE1) while activating the beta- secretase ADAM10 and up-regulating sAPPalpha, thus shifting the cleavage of betaAPP holoenzyme from an amyloidogenic into the non- amyloidogenic pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["Prostaglandin subgraph", "Neuroprotection subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21246057", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21246057"}}, {start: {_id:2678}, end: {_id:2680}, properties:{evidence:"We also show that NPD1 downregulates Abeta42-triggered expression of the pro-inflammatory enzyme cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) and of B-94 (a TNF-alpha-inducible pro-inflammatory element) and apoptosis in HNG cells. Moreover, NPD1 suppresses Abeta42 peptide shedding by down- regulating beta-secretase-1 (BACE1) while activating the beta- secretase ADAM10 and up-regulating sAPPalpha, thus shifting the cleavage of betaAPP holoenzyme from an amyloidogenic into the non- amyloidogenic pathway.", annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21246057", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21246057"}}, {start: {_id:2678}, end: {_id:20}, properties:{evidence:"We also show that NPD1 downregulates Abeta42-triggered expression of the pro-inflammatory enzyme cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) and of B-94 (a TNF-alpha-inducible pro-inflammatory element) and apoptosis in HNG cells. Moreover, NPD1 suppresses Abeta42 peptide shedding by down- regulating beta-secretase-1 (BACE1) while activating the beta- secretase ADAM10 and up-regulating sAPPalpha, thus shifting the cleavage of betaAPP holoenzyme from an amyloidogenic into the non- amyloidogenic pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph", "Neuroprotection subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21246057", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21246057"}}, {start: {_id:2678}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"In human neural cells overexpressing beta-amyloid precursor protein (betaAPP), the lipid mediator suppressed Abeta42 shedding by downregulating beta-secretase (BACE1) while activating the alpha- secretase (ADAM10), thus shifting the alphaAPP cleavage from the noxious amyloidogenic pathway into a non-amyloidogenic, neurotrophic pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Beta secretase subgraph", "Neuroprotection subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21431475", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21431475"}}, {start: {_id:2678}, end: {_id:20}, properties:{evidence:"In human neural cells overexpressing beta-amyloid precursor protein (betaAPP), the lipid mediator suppressed Abeta42 shedding by downregulating beta-secretase (BACE1) while activating the alpha- secretase (ADAM10), thus 