Type:"PubMed", citationRef:"18217188"}}, {start: {_id:3422}, end: {_id:174}, properties:{evidence:"Neuronal injury-induced glial apoE secretion is attenuated by the nuclear factor kappaB inhibitors, aspirin, Bay 11-7082 and MG-132, suggesting that this transcription factor is involved in both constitutive and induced glial apoE expression", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], pmid:"11311545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11311545"}}, {start: {_id:3423}, end: {_id:174}, properties:{evidence:"Neuronal injury-induced glial apoE secretion is attenuated by the nuclear factor kappaB inhibitors, aspirin, Bay 11-7082 and MG-132, suggesting that this transcription factor is involved in both constitutive and induced glial apoE expression", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], pmid:"11311545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11311545"}}, {start: {_id:3424}, end: {_id:174}, properties:{evidence:"Neuronal injury-induced glial apoE secretion is attenuated by the nuclear factor kappaB inhibitors, aspirin, Bay 11-7082 and MG-132, suggesting that this transcription factor is involved in both constitutive and induced glial apoE expression", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], pmid:"11311545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11311545"}}, {start: {_id:3425}, end: {_id:174}, properties:{evidence:"Neuronal injury-induced glial apoE secretion is attenuated by the nuclear factor kappaB inhibitors, aspirin, Bay 11-7082 and MG-132, suggesting that this transcription factor is involved in both constitutive and induced glial apoE expression", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], pmid:"11311545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11311545"}}, {start: {_id:3422}, end: {_id:1926}, properties:{evidence:"Neuronal injury-induced glial apoE secretion is attenuated by the nuclear factor kappaB inhibitors, aspirin, Bay 11-7082 and MG-132, suggesting that this transcription factor is involved in both constitutive and induced glial apoE expression", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], pmid:"11311545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11311545"}}, {start: {_id:3531}, end: {_id:3330}, properties:{evidence:"Furthermore, treatment with liquiritigenin inhibited astrocytosis in the hippocampus, and it may through its inhibitory activities on Notch-2, an important molecular regulating neural proliferation and differentiation. These findings provide evidence for beneficial activity of liquiritigenin in a mouse model of Alzheimer's disease and support the continued investigation of Notch signaling pathway as a target for treatment of Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21872584", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21872584"}}, {start: {_id:3531}, end: {_id:3330}, properties:{evidence:"Our results show that liquiritigenin treatment improves the behavioral performance of the model rats and attenuates neuronal loss in the brain. More importantly, liquiritigenin treatment decreases mRNA levels and protein expression of Notch-2, an effect that could promote the generation of new neurons. These findings provide evidence for the beneficial activity of liquiritigenin in a brain-injured rat model and support the continued investigation of SERMs such as liquiritigenin as an alternative to estrogen-based hormone therapy in reducing the risk of neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Notch signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20117143", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20117143"}}, {start: {_id:3}, end: {_id:1751}, properties:{evidence:"Physiological levels of ABeta¸ also have trophic and neuroprotective actions in trophic deprived conditions [95]. Many ABeta¸ has a physiological role in normal synapse function. In organotypic hippocampal slices, BACE activity is increased by synaptic activity and the resulting ABeta¸ peptides depress excitatory transmission through a-amino-3-hydroxyl-5-methyl-4-isoxazole-propionate (AMPA) and N-methyl-D-aspartic acid (NMDA) receptors, suggesting a role for ABeta¸ in homeostatic plasticity [29]. ABeta¸ may have an important physiological role in synapse elimination during brain development", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:3}, end: {_id:1752}, properties:{evidence:"Physiological levels of ABeta¸ also have trophic and neuroprotective actions in trophic deprived conditions [95]. Many ABeta¸ has a physiological role in normal synapse function. In organotypic hippocampal slices, BACE activity is increased by synaptic activity and the resulting ABeta¸ peptides depress excitatory transmission through a-amino-3-hydroxyl-5-methyl-4-isoxazole-propionate (AMPA) and N-methyl-D-aspartic acid (NMDA) receptors, suggesting a role for ABeta¸ in homeostatic plasticity [29]. ABeta¸ may have an important physiological role in synapse elimination during brain development", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:3}, end: {_id:1753}, properties:{evidence:"Physiological levels of ABeta¸ also have trophic and neuroprotective actions in trophic deprived conditions [95]. Many ABeta¸ has a physiological role in normal synapse function. In organotypic hippocampal slices, BACE activity is increased by synaptic activity and the resulting ABeta¸ peptides depress excitatory transmission through a-amino-3-hydroxyl-5-methyl-4-isoxazole-propionate (AMPA) and N-methyl-D-aspartic acid (NMDA) receptors, suggesting a role for ABeta¸ in homeostatic plasticity [29]. ABeta¸ may have an important physiological role in synapse elimination during brain development", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:3}, end: {_id:1754}, properties:{evidence:"Physiological levels of ABeta¸ also have trophic and neuroprotective actions in trophic deprived conditions [95]. Many ABeta¸ has a physiological role in normal synapse function. In organotypic hippocampal slices, BACE activity is increased by synaptic activity and the resulting ABeta¸ peptides depress excitatory transmission through a-amino-3-hydroxyl-5-methyl-4-isoxazole-propionate (AMPA) and N-methyl-D-aspartic acid (NMDA) receptors, suggesting a role for ABeta¸ in homeostatic plasticity [29]. ABeta¸ may have an important physiological role in synapse elimination during brain development", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}] AS row
