llular glucose, acetylcholine, cholesterol, and ATP associated with abnormalities in membrane pathology and the formation of both amyloidogenic derivatives and hyperphosphorylated tau protein.", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"11956956", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11956956"}}, {start: {_id:2337}, end: {_id:3}, properties:{evidence:"Deficiency of ABCC1 substantially increased cerebral Abeta levels without altering the expression of most enzymes that would favor the production of Abeta from the Abeta precursor protein.", annotationSubgraph:["ATP binding cassette transport subgraph", "Non-amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21881209", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21881209"}}, {start: {_id:122}, end: {_id:527}, properties:{evidence:"In the present review, we discuss our initial in vitro results and additional investigations showing that Abeta activates GSK-3beta through impairment of phosphatidylinositol-3 (PI3)/Akt signaling; that Abeta-activated GSK-3beta induces hyperphosphorylation of tau, NFT formation, neuronal death, and synaptic loss (all found in the AD brain); that GSK-3beta can induce memory deficits in vivo; and that inhibition of GSK-3alpha (an isoform of GSK-3beta) reduces Abeta production.", annotationSubgraph:["Tau protein subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph", "GSK3 subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"16914869", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16914869"}}, {start: {_id:3563}, end: {_id:3564}, properties:{evidence:"Complex I 24-kDa subunit was significantly reduced in occipital cortex and thalamus in patients with DS and temporal and occipital cortices in patients with AD. Complex I 75-kDa subunit was significantly reduced in brain regions from patients with DS (temporal, occipital and caudate nucleus) and AD (parietal cortex). Reductions of two subunits of complex I may lead to the impairment of energy metabolism and result in neuronal cell death (apoptotic process), a hallmark of both neurodegenerative disorders.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"11400916", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11400916"}}, {start: {_id:3563}, end: {_id:3565}, properties:{evidence:"Complex I 24-kDa subunit was significantly reduced in occipital cortex and thalamus in patients with DS and temporal and occipital cortices in patients with AD. Complex I 75-kDa subunit was significantly reduced in brain regions from patients with DS (temporal, occipital and caudate nucleus) and AD (parietal cortex). Reductions of two subunits of complex I may lead to the impairment of energy metabolism and result in neuronal cell death (apoptotic process), a hallmark of both neurodegenerative disorders.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"11400916", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11400916"}}, {start: {_id:3566}, end: {_id:3567}, properties:{evidence:"Complex I 24-kDa subunit was significantly reduced in occipital cortex and thalamus in patients with DS and temporal and occipital cortices in patients with AD. Complex I 75-kDa subunit was significantly reduced in brain regions from patients with DS (temporal, occipital and caudate nucleus) and AD (parietal cortex). Reductions of two subunits of complex I may lead to the impairment of energy metabolism and result in neuronal cell death (apoptotic process), a hallmark of both neurodegenerative disorders.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"11400916", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11400916"}}, {start: {_id:4474}, end: {_id:2041}, properties:{evidence:"a significant decrease in Hcy levels was paralleled by a significant increase in MAT activity", annotationSubgraph:["Epigenetic modification sub