 abilities of the Alzheimer's disease-like rats, possibly by improving the function of the acetylcholine system and decreasing the astrocytes proliferation in the brain.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Astrocytes", "Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21158163", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21158163"}}, {start: {_id:2329}, end: {_id:796}, properties:{evidence:"One selective inducer of ADAM10 gene expression is the X-box binding protein-1 (XBP-1).", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Unfolded protein response subgraph"], pmid:"24165480", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24165480"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:2350}, properties:{evidence:"In this study we demonstrate that uPAR mRNA and protein expression is induced following incubation of human post-mortem brain-derived microglia with fibrillar amyloid beta (Abeta) peptide.", annotationSubgraph:["Plasminogen activator subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Microglia"], pmid:"11814408", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11814408"}}, {start: {_id:115}, end: {_id:2375}, properties:{evidence:"Interestingly, as a negative feedback mechanism, Cdk5 phosphorylates and suppresses NOS1 activity.", annotationSubgraph:["Cyclin-CDK subgraph", "Nitric oxide subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22874667", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22874667"}}, {start: {_id:1917}, end: {_id:2393}, properties:{evidence:"Acrolein induces Hsp72 via both PKCdelta/JNK and calcium signaling pathways in human umbilical vein endothelial cells.", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph", "JAK-STAT signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationCell:["endothelial cell of umbilical vein"], pmid:"16036326", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16036326"}}, {start: {_id:1917}, end: {_id:2393}, properties:{evidence:"Here, we report that low concentrations of acrolein induce Hsp72 in human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC) and that both the PKCdelta/JNK pathway and calcium pathway were involved in the induction.", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph", "JAK-STAT signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationCell:["endothelial cell of umbilical vein"], pmid:"16036326", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16036326"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:2385}, properties:{evidence:"We found that the kinase activity of PKC-delta but not that of PKC- alpha or -epsilon is increased by stimulation of microglia with Abeta, with a striking tyrosine phosphorylation of PKC-delta.", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Microglia"], pmid:"11290384", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11290384"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:762}, properties:{evidence:"Taken together with our previous observations that Abeta-induced phosphorylation of MARCKS and chemotaxis of microglia are inhibited by either tyrosine kinase or PKC inhibitors, our results provide evidence that Abeta induces phosphorylation and translocation of MARCKS through the tyrosine kinase-PKC-delta signaling pathway in microglia.", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Microglia"], pmid:"11290384", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11290384"}}, {start: {_id:2431}, end: {_id:263}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, 5-LO targeted gene disruption or its in vivo selective pharmacological inhibition results in a significant reduction of Abeta, CREB and gamma-secretase levels. These data establish a novel functional role for 5-LO in regulating endogenous formation of Abeta levels in the central nervous system.", annotationSubgraph:["CREB subgraph", "Eicosanoids signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Central Nervous System"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21280074", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21280074"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:2456}, properties:{evidence:"We have previously reported that CysLT1R activation is involved in Abeta generation. In this study, we investigated rescuing effect of CysLT1R antagonist montelukast on Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity in primary neurons. Our data showed that Abeta1-42 elicited a marked increase of CysLT1R expression in primary mouse neurons. This increment of CysLT1R expression was accompanied by increases of inflammatory factors such as NF-ÎºB p65, tumor necrosis factor-Î± (TNFÎ±) and interleukin-1beta (IL-1beta) as well as pro-apoptotic protein Caspase-3 activation and anti-apoptotic process protein Bcl-2 reduction. This observation was confirmed with treatment of montelukast, a selective CysLT1R antagonist, which had significant effect on Abeta1-42-induced cytotoxicity. Moreover, blockade of CysLT1R with montelukast reversed Abeta1-42-mediated increase of CysLT1R expression, and concomitant changes of the pro-inflammatory factors and the apoptotic process-related proteins. The results demonstrate that montelukast rescued neurons against Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity, neuroinflammation and apoptosis by down-regulating CysLT1R-mediated NF-ÎºB signaling, suggesting that CysLT1R may be a potential target for AD, and its antagonist may have beneficial effects for treatment of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24879954", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24879954"}}, {start: {_id:2456}, end: {_id:2458}, properties:{evidence:"We have previously reported that CysLT1R activation is involved in Abeta generation. In this study, we investigated rescuing effect of CysLT1R antagonist montelukast on Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity in primary neurons. Our data showed that Abeta1-42 elicited a marked increase of CysLT1R expression in primary mouse neurons. This increment of CysLT1R expression was accompanied by increases of inflammatory factors such as NF-ÎºB p65, tumor necrosis factor-Î± (TNFÎ±) and interleukin-1beta (IL-1beta) as well as pro-apoptotic protein Caspase-3 activation and anti-apoptotic process protein Bcl-2 reduction. This observation was confirmed with treatment of montelukast, a selective CysLT1R antagonist, which had significant effect on Abeta1-42-induced cytotoxicity. Moreover, blockade of CysLT1R with montelukast reversed Abeta1-42-mediated increase of CysLT1R expression, and concomitant changes of the pro-inflammatory factors and the apoptotic process-related proteins. The results demonstrate that montelukast rescued neurons against Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity, neuroinflammation and apoptosis by down-regulating CysLT1R-mediated NF-ÎºB signaling, suggesting that CysLT1R may be a potential target for AD, and its antagonist may have beneficial effects for treatment of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24879954", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24879954"}}, {start: {_id:2456}, end: {_id:2459}, properties:{evidence:"We have previously reported that CysLT1R activation is involved in Abeta generation. In this study, we investigated rescuing effect of CysLT1R antagonist montelukast on Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity in primary neurons. Our data showed that Abeta1-42 elicited a marked increase of CysLT1R expression in primary mouse neurons. This increment of CysLT1R expression was accompanied by increases of inflammatory factors such as NF-ÎºB p65, tumor necrosis factor-Î± (TNFÎ±) and interleukin-1beta (IL-1beta) as well as pro-apoptotic protein Caspase-3 activation and anti-apoptotic process protein Bcl-2 reduction. This observation was confirmed with treatment of montelukast, a selective CysLT1R antagonist, which had significant effect on Abeta1-42-induced cytotoxicity. Moreover, blockade of CysLT1R with montelukast reversed Abeta1-42-mediated increase of CysLT1R expression, and concomitant changes of the pro-inflammatory factors and the apoptotic process-related proteins. The results demonstrate that montelukast rescued neurons against Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity, neuroinflammation and apoptosis by down-regulating CysLT1R-mediated NF-ÎºB signaling, suggesting that CysLT1R may be a potential target for AD, and its antagonist may have beneficial effects for treatment of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24879954", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24879954"}}, {start: {_id:2456}, end: {_id:2029}, properties:{evidence:"We have previously reported that CysLT1R activation is involved in Abeta generation. In this study, we investigated rescuing effect of CysLT1R antagonist montelukast on Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity in primary neurons. Our data showed that Abeta1-42 elicited a marked increase of CysLT1R expression in primary mouse neurons. This increment of CysLT1R expression was accompanied by increases of inflammatory factors such as NF-ÎºB p65, tumor necrosis factor-Î± (TNFÎ±) and interleukin-1beta (IL-1beta) as well as pro-apoptotic protein Caspase-3 activation and anti-apoptotic process protein Bcl-2 reduction. This observation was confirmed with treatment of montelukast, a selective CysLT1R antagonist, which had significant effect on Abeta1-42-induced cytotoxicity. Moreover, blockade of CysLT1R with montelukast reversed Abeta1-42-mediated increase of CysLT1R expression, and concomitant changes of the pro-inflammatory factors and the apoptotic process-related proteins. The results demonstrate that montelukast rescued neurons against Abeta1-42-induced neurotoxicity, neuroinflammation and apoptosis by down-regulating CysLT1R-mediated NF-ÎºB signaling, suggesting that CysLT1R may be a potential target for AD, and its antagonist may have beneficial effects for treatment of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24879954", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24879954"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2471}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, pericytes respond to the pro-inflammatory cytokines tumor necrosis factor-Î± and Interferon-gamma by inducing the expression of the CYP27B1 gene which is involved in 1,25D synthesis.", annotationSubgraph:["Vitamin subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph", "Metabolism of steroid hormones subgraph"], pmid:"24934545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24934545"}}, {start: {_id:224}, end: {_id:2471}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, pericytes respond to the pro-inflammatory cytokines tumor necrosis factor-Î± and Interferon-gamma by inducing the expression of the CYP27B1 gene which is involved in 1,25D synthesis.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph", "Vitamin subgraph", "Metabolism of steroid hormones subgraph"], pmid:"24934545", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24934545"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2477}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2478}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2479}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2480}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2481}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2482}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2483}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2484}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:2486}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:1751}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:96}, end: {_id:462}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, TNF-alpha can potentiate glutamate-mediated cytotoxicity by two complementary mechanisms: indirectly, by inhibiting glutamate transport on astrocytes, and directly, by rapidly triggering the surface expression of Ca+2 permeable-AMPA receptors and NMDA receptors, while decreasing inhibitory GABAA receptors on neurons.", annotationSubgraph:["Glutamatergic subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24966471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24966471"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:2493}, properties:{evidence:"In Alzheimer's disease (AD), affected neurons accumulate beta amyloid protein, components of which can induce mouse microglia to express the high-output isoform of nitric oxide synthase (NOS2) in vitro. Products of NOS2 can be neurotoxic. In mice, NOS2 is normally suppressed by transforming growth factor beta 1 (TGF-beta 1). Expression of TGF-beta 1 is decreased in brains from AD patients, a situation that might be permissive for accumulation of NOS2.", annotationSubgraph:["Nitric oxide subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Microglia"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8879214", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8879214"}}, {start: {_id:2508}, end: {_id:1681}, properties:{evidence:"Distinct from WT SOD1, mutant SOD1 induces morphological change and cytochrome c release in cultured neurons that resulted in apoptotic process. Two transgenic studies further indicated the involvement of mitochondria-mediated apoptotic process in mutant SOD1-linked ALS.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Neurons"], pmid:"22072931", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22072931"}}, {start: {_id:2527}, end: {_id:601}, properties:{evidence:"We showed that the localization of mutant SOD1 in the mitochondria triggered the release of mitochondrial cytochrome c followed by the activation of caspase cascade and induced neuronal cell death without cytoplasmic mutant SOD1 aggregate formation.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"12393885", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12393885"}}, {start: {_id:2508}, end: {_id:1678}, properties:{evidence:"In contrast, in symptomatic mice, expression of Bcl-2 and Bcl-XL, which inhibit apoptotic process, is reduced, whereas expression of Bad and Bax, which stimulate apoptotic process, is increased. These alterations are specific to affected brain regions and are caused by the mutant and not by the normal SOD1 enzyme.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Spinal Cord"], pmid:"10582606", annotationDisease:["amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10582606"}}, {start: {_id:2508}, end: {_id:2529}, properties:{evidence:"In contrast, in symptomatic mice, expression of Bcl-2 and Bcl-XL, which inhibit apoptotic process, is reduced, whereas expression of Bad and Bax, which stimulate apoptotic process, is increased. These alterations are specific to affected brain regions and are caused by the mutant and not by the normal SOD1 enzyme.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Spinal Cord"], pmid:"10582606", annotationDisease:["amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10582606"}}, {start: {_id:1678}, end: {_id:1681}, properties:{evidence:"Overexpression of Bax induces the release of cytochrome c, which activates Apaf-1, which is associated caspase-independent activity. Caspase-3 activity is an important signaling molecule in apoptosis and affects the function of mitochondria by ROS and NO. Over-expression of Bax protein, followed by up-regulated activity of caspase-3 induces cell death", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], pmid:"25009706", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25009706"}}, {start: {_id:404}, end: {_id:601}, properties:{evidence:"Furthermore, mitochondrial ceramide generation induces intrinsic apoptosis mediated by cytochrome c release. However, all these apoptotic events can be prevented by overexpression of Bcl-2s", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], pmid:"23455468", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23455468"}}, {start: {_id:1119}, end: {_id:796}, properties:{evidence:"Some authors show that IGF-I increases Î±-secretase processing of endogenous amyloid precursor protein and the amyloid precursor-like proteins 1 and 2 [36, 97-100].", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22524398", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22524398"}}, {start: {_id:796}, end: {_id:798}, properties:{evidence:"Some authors show that IGF-I increases Î±-secretase processing of endogenous amyloid precursor protein and the amyloid precursor-like proteins 1 and 2 [36, 97-100].", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Insulin signal transduction"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22524398", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22524398"}}, {start: {_id:122}, end: {_id:2594}, properties:{evidence:"Here, we show that GSK-3beta phosphorylates and regulates the activity of Bax, a pro-apoptotic Bcl-2 family member that stimulates the intrinsic (mitochondrial) death pathway by eliciting cytochrome c release from mitochondria.", annotationSubgraph:["GSK3 subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"15525785", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15525785"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:97}, end: {_id:798}, properties:{evidence:"we unexpectedly found that IL-1beta significantly enhanced alpha- cleavage, indicated by increases in sAPPalpha and C83, but reduced beta-cleavage, indicated by decreases in sAPPbeta and Abeta40/42, in human neuroblastoma SK-N-SH cells. IL-1beta did not significantly alter the mRNA levels of BACE1, ADAM-9, and ADAM-10, but up-regulated that of TACE by threefold.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"18021299", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18021299"}}, {start: {_id:2643}, end: {_id:798}, properties:{evidence:"The overexpression of ADAM19 in HEK293 cells resulted in an increase in sAPPalpha. Therefore, we suggest that ADAM19 has a constitutive alpha-secretase activity.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Non-amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17112471", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17112471"}}, {start: {_id:2647}, end: {_id:2652}, properties:{evidence:"Previous experiments indicate that RAGE mediates A beta-induced oxidative stress and nuclear factor-kB activation (Yan et al., 1996) as well as neuronal expression of macrophage colony-stimulating factor (Du Yan et al., 1997), mitogen-activated protein (MAP) kinases signaling defects (Arancio et al., 2004), or cell death (Hadding et al., 2004).", annotationSubgraph:["Immunoglobulin subgraph", "Cytokine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18480271", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18480271"}}, {start: {_id:2647}, end: {_id:20}, properties:{evidence:"RAGE stimulates functional BACE1 expression through NFAT1 activation, resulting in more Abeta production and deposition in the brain.", annotationSubgraph:["Immunoglobulin subgraph", "Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph", "T cells signaling"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"19332646", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19332646"}}, {start: {_id:2659}, end: {_id:2661}, properties:{evidence:"Recently, GRK2 and GRK5 have been demonstrated to phosphorylate alpha- synuclein (Ser129) and other synuclein isoforms.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17146290", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17146290"}}, {start: {_id:2662}, end: {_id:2661}, properties:{evidence:"Recently, GRK2 and GRK5 have been demonstrated to phosphorylate alpha- synuclein (Ser129) and other synuclein isoforms.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17146290", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17146290"}}, {start: {_id:413}, end: {_id:218}, properties:{evidence:"We hypothesized LRP2 may be involved in efflux of apoJ out of the CNS, and Abeta binding to apoJ may enhance clearance of highly pathogenic Abeta42. Our data show that both RAP and LRP2-specific antibody block apoJ clearance, indicating LRP2 is required for apoJ efflux at the BBB.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Endosomal lysosomal subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17077814", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17077814"}}, {start: {_id:205}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"The fact that acetylcholinesterase accelerates amyloid formation and the effect is sensitive to peripherical anionic site blockers of the enzyme, suggests that specific and new EC 3.1.1.7 (acetylcholinesterase) inhibitors may well provide an attractive possibility for treating Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18205831", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18205831"}}, {start: {_id:205}, end: {_id:666}, properties:{evidence:"Recent studies also indicate that acetylcholinesterase induces the aggregation of prion protein with a similar dependence on the peripherical anionic site", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18205831", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18205831"}}, {start: {_id:20}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"In human neural cells overexpressing beta-amyloid precursor protein (betaAPP), the lipid mediator suppressed Abeta42 shedding by downregulating beta-secretase (BACE1) while activating the alpha- secretase (ADAM10), thus shifting the alphaAPP cleavage from the noxious amyloidogenic pathway into a non-amyloidogenic, neurotrophic pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Beta secretase subgraph", "Neuroprotection subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21431475", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21431475"}}, {start: {_id:796}, end: {_id:798}, properties:{evidence:"In human neural cells overexpressing beta-amyloid precursor protein (betaAPP), the lipid mediator suppressed Abeta42 shedding by downregulating beta-secretase (BACE1) while activating the alpha- secretase (ADAM10), thus shifting the alphaAPP cleavage from the noxious amyloidogenic pathway into a non-amyloidogenic, neurotrophic pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Beta secretase subgraph", "Neuroprotection subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21431475", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21431475"}}, {start: {_id:97}, end: {_id:992}, properties:{evidence:"IL-1beta did not significantly alter the mRNA levels of BACE1, ADAM-9, and ADAM-10,but up-regulated that of TACE by threefold. The proform and mature form of TACE protein were also significantly up-regulated.", annotationSubgraph:["ADAM Metallopeptidase subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18021299", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18021299"}}, {start: {_id:886}, end: {_id:257}, properties:{evidence:"In each case, the Abstimulation of M-CSF secretion was significantly blocked by treatment of cultures with anti-RAGE F(ab')2.", annotationSubgraph:["Cytokine signaling subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"15882940", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15882940"}}, {start: {_id:863}, end: {_id:150}, properties:{evidence:"These studies indicate that agrin is capable of accelerating the formation of insoluble protein fibrils in a second common neurodegenerative disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Synuclein subgraph"], pmid:"16037493", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16037493"}}, {start: {_id:174}, end: {_id:2719}, properties:{evidence:"As LRP ligands associated with Abeta deposits in AD brain may play an important role in inducing levels of LRP in both neurons and astrocytes, our findings support the idea that apoE might be involved in upregulation of LRP (present in fine astrocytic processes) and act as a local scaffolding protein for LRP and Abeta.", annotationSubgraph:["Low density lipoprotein subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"12117549", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"12117549"}}, {start: {_id:2725}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"We further demonstrated that apolipoprotein E2 and E3 but not apolipoprotein E4 can decrease the fusogenic activity of Abeta(29-42) via a direct interaction.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"10428074", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10428074"}}, {start: {_id:2736}, end: {_id:20}, properties:{evidence:"Taken together, our results strongly suggest that furin, or a furin- like proprotein convertase, is responsible for cleaving the BACE propeptide domain to form the mature enzyme.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"10956649", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10956649"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:20}, properties:{evidence:"Expression of familial Alzheimer's disease (FAD) mutants of APP in primary neurons causes both intracellular accumulation of the C-terminal beta-secretase cleavage product of APP and increased secretion of Abeta, and eventually results in apoptotic death of the cells.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"11744168", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11744168"}}, {start: {_id:224}, end: {_id:96}, properties:{evidence:"Aggregated Abeta induced IFN-gamma production from co-culture of astrocytes and microglia, and IFN-gamma elicited tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha secretion in wild type (WT) but not GRKO microglia co- cultured with astrocytes.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17255335", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17255335"}}, {start: {_id:2075}, end: {_id:20}, properties:{evidence:"Specifically, hypoxia significantly increases beta-site APP cleaving enzyme (BACE1) gene transcription through the over-expression of hypoxia inducible factor 1alpha, resulting in increased BACE1 secretase activity and amyloid-beta production.", annotationSubgraph:["Hypoxia response subgraph", "Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"19196431", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19196431"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
