UNWIND [{start: {_id:295}, end: {_id:296}, properties:{annotationCellstructure:["Mitochondria"], evidence:"Interestingly, in brain tissues of AD-affected subjects, APP localized with mitochondria fraction, associated to TOM40 and TIM23, in a translocation-arrested manner, that may prevent import of de novo synthesised nuclear-encoded mitochondrial protein,such as subunits of the electron transport chain", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial translocation subgraph", "Electron transport chain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22523685", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22523685"}}, {start: {_id:295}, end: {_id:73}, properties:{annotationCellstructure:["Mitochondria"], evidence:"Interestingly, in brain tissues of AD-affected subjects, APP localized with mitochondria fraction, associated to TOM40 and TIM23, in a translocation-arrested manner, that may prevent import of de novo synthesised nuclear-encoded mitochondrial protein,such as subunits of the electron transport chain", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial translocation subgraph", "Electron transport chain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22523685", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22523685"}}, {start: {_id:302}, end: {_id:291}, properties:{annotationCellstructure:["Mitochondria"], evidence:"In support of the hypothesis that APP and amyloid-beta enter mitochondria, several studies have found APP and its derivatives (monomeric and oligomeric forms of amyloid-beta) in mitochondrial membranes. Amyloid-beta normally interact with the mitochondrial matrix protein, amyloid-beta-binding alcohol dehydrogenase (ABAD), leading to mitochondrial dysfunction", annotationSubgraph:["Apoptosis signaling subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22523685", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22523685"}}, {start: {_id:526}, end: {_id:34}, properties:{evidence:"We recently reported increased mitochondrial fission and decreased fusion, increased amyloid beta (Abeta) interaction with the mitochondrial fission protein Drp1, increased mitochondrial fragmentation, impaired axonal transport of mitochondria and synaptic degeneration in neurons affected by AD. In the present study, we extended our previous investigations to determine whether phosphorylated tau interacts with Drp1 and to elucidate mitochondrial damage in the progression of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Axonal transport subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22367970", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22367970"}}, {start: {_id:667}, end: {_id:668}, properties:{evidence:"Abeta42 oligomers bind to PrP with high affinity and specificity. Abeta42 either did not bind to RAGE or the a7 nicotinic acetylcholine receptor or bound with very low affinity. Nanomolar concentrations of oligomeric Abeta42 potently inhibited LTP, but this effect could be abrogated either in the absence of PrP or by blocking the Abeta42-PrP interaction with antibodies against the Abeta42-binding region. Thus, the authors provide compelling evidence that PrP is a specific binding partner for Abeta42 oligomers and mediates Abeta42â€™s inhibitory effect on synaptic plasticity. This finding fits well with previous reports that independently implicate Abeta42 and PrP in synaptic function", annotationSubgraph:["Synapse assembly subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"18650430", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18650430"}}, {start: {_id:321}, end: {_id:290}, properties:{evidence:"Mitochondrial Abeta-binding alcohol dehydrogenase (ABAD): ABAD is a member of the short chain dehydrogenase reductase family in mitochondria that binds Abeta. Binding of Abeta to ABAD distorts the enzymebetas structure, rendering it inactive. In neurons, ABAD is predominately localized to mitochondria. Upon binding ABAD, Abeta triggers events leading to neuronal apoptosis through a mitochondrial pathway.Interestingly, mitochondrial ABAD is upregulated in neurons from AD patients. The ABAD-Abeta complex has been hypothesized to induce oxidant stress and mitochondrial dysfunction. Increased expression of ABAD exacerbates Abeta-mediated mitochondrial and neuronal stress. Abeta binding to ABAD causes free radical production and neuronal apoptotic process", annotationSubgraph:["Mitochondrial translocation subgraph", "Metabolism of steroid hormones subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:2360}, end: {_id:98}, properties:{evidence:"X-linked inhibitor of apoptosis protein (XIAP) blocks the apoptosis by binding to and inhibiting caspases-3, 7, and 9. XIAP is negatively regulated by the mitochondrial serine protease, HtrA2/Omi.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph", "XIAP subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22273571", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22273571"}}, {start: {_id:2365}, end: {_id:2366}, properties:{evidence:"Here, we report that nitric oxide (NO) reacts with XIAP by S-nitrosylating its RING domain (forming SNO-XIAP), thereby inhibiting E3 ligase and antiapoptotic activity.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin degradation subgraph", "XIAP subgraph", "Nitric oxide subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"20670888", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20670888"}}, {start: {_id:2645}, end: {_id:2702}, properties:{evidence:"Interaction of mitochondrial Abeta with mitochondrial enzymes such as amyloid beta binding alcohol dehydrogenase (ABAD) exaggerates mitochondrial stress by inhibiting the enzyme activity, releasing reactive oxygen species (ROS), and affecting glycolytic, Krebs cycle and/or the respiratory chain pathways through the accumulation of deleterious intermediate metabolites.", annotationSubgraph:["Alcohol dehydrogenase subgraph", "Reactive oxygen species subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17424907", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17424907"}}, {start: {_id:2884}, end: {_id:98}, properties:{evidence:"Clusterin is a stress-induced chaperone which is normally secreted but in conditions of cellular stress, it can be transported to cytoplasm where it can bind to Bax protein and inhibit neuronal apoptotic process.", annotationSubgraph:["Endosomal lysosomal subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], pmid:"16113678", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16113678"}}, {start: {_id:2856}, end: {_id:2370}, properties:{evidence:"Twenty years ago, it was demonstrated that the expression of clusterin was clearly increased in Alzheimer's disease (AD). Later it was observed that clusterin can bind amyloid-beta peptides and prevent their fibrillization.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "Endosomal lysosomal subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"19651157", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19651157"}}, {start: {_id:3517}, end: {_id:848}, properties:{evidence:"These results reveal a link between Munc18-1 and Dyrk1A in synaptic vesicle trafficking and amyloid precursor protein processing, suggesting that up-regulated Dyrk1A in Down's syndrome and Alzheimer's disease brains may contribute to some pathological features, including synaptic dysfunction and cognitive defect through abnormal phosphorylation of Munc18-1.", annotationSubgraph:["DYRK1A subgraph"], pmid:"22765017", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22765017"}}, {start: {_id:3517}, end: {_id:193}, properties:{evidence:"These results reveal a link between Munc18-1 and Dyrk1A in synaptic vesicle trafficking and amyloid precursor protein processing, suggesting that up-regulated Dyrk1A in Down's syndrome and Alzheimer's disease brains may contribute to some pathological features, including synaptic dysfunction and cognitive defect through abnormal phosphorylation of Munc18-1.", annotationSubgraph:["DYRK1A subgraph"], pmid:"22765017", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22765017"}}, {start: {_id:3518}, end: {_id:3519}, properties:{evidence:"Cyclin-dependent kinase 5 and its activator p35 disrupt Munc18a-syntaxin 1 binding, thereby promoting synaptic vesicle fusion during exocytosis. We investigated protein levels of the signaling pathway: p35, cyclin-dependent kinase 5, Munc18a, syntaxin 1A and 1B, Munc18-interacting protein 1 and Munc18-interacting protein 2 in Alzheimer's disease cortex and found that this pathway was up- regulated in the Alzheimer's disease parietal and occipital cortex.", annotationSubgraph:["Cyclin-CDK subgraph"], pmid:"16413130", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16413130"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:DECREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:123}, end: {_id:3602}, properties:{evidence:"Abeta-Amyloid (Abeta) plaques in Alzheimer (AD) brains are surrounded by severe dendritic and axonal changes, including local spine loss, axonal swellings and distorted neurite trajectories. Whether and how plaques induce these neuropil abnormalities remains unknown. We tested the hypothesis that oligomeric assemblies of Abeta, seen in the periphery of plaques, mediate the neurodegenerative phenotype of AD by triggering activation of the enzyme GSK-3beta, which in turn appears to inhibit a transcriptional program mediated by CREB. We detect increased activity of GSK-3beta after exposure to oligomeric Abeta in neurons in culture, in the brain of double transgenic APP/tau mice and in AD brains. Activation of GSK-3beta, even in the absence of Abeta, is sufficient to produce a phenocopy of Abeta-induced dendritic spine loss in neurons in culture, while pharmacological inhibition of GSK-3beta prevents spine loss and increases expression of CREB-target genes like BDNF. Of note, in transgenic mice GSK-3beta inhibition ameliorated plaque-related neuritic changes and increased CREB- mediated gene expression. Moreover, GSK-3beta inhibition robustly decreased the oligomeric Abeta load in the mouse brain. All these findings support the idea that GSK3beta is aberrantly activated by the presence of Abeta, and contributes, at least in part, to the neuronal anatomical derangement associated with Abeta plaques in AD brains and to Abeta pathology itself.", annotationSubgraph:["GSK3 subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21945540", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21945540"}}, {start: {_id:4232}, end: {_id:4233}, properties:{evidence:"Further, Runx1t1 and HDACs were shown to promote neurotoxic effect of microglia by repressing expression of LAT2, L-aminoacid transporter-2 (cationic amino acid transporter, y+ system), which normally inhibits NO production.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Microglia"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24586690", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24586690"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:DECREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
