ic polypeptide, insulinlike growth factor binding protein 2, beta(2) microglobulin, vascular cell adhesion molecule 1, carcinoembryonic antigen, matrix metalloprotein 2, CD40, macrophage inflammatory protein 1Î±, superoxide dismutase, and homocysteine) and decreased (apolipoprotein E, epidermal growth factor receptor, hemoglobin, calcium, zinc, interleukin 17, and albumin) in AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Cell adhesion subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Plasma"], pmid:"22801742", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22801742"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2078}, properties:{evidence:"Brain sections from AD and control mice showed that HIF-1α, Ang-2, MMP2 and caspase 3 are elevated and Bcl-xL decreased in the microvasculature of AD mice.", annotationSubgraph:["Hypoxia response subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], pmid:"21904637", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21904637"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2028}, properties:{evidence:"Brain sections from AD and control mice showed that HIF-1α, Ang-2, MMP2 and caspase 3 are elevated and Bcl-xL decreased in the microvasculature of AD mice.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], pmid:"21904637", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21904637"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1680}, properties:{evidence:"Brain sections from AD and control mice showed that HIF-1α, Ang-2, MMP2 and caspase 3 are elevated and Bcl-xL decreased in the microvasculature of AD mice.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], pmid:"21904637", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21904637"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2081}, properties:{evidence:"Osteopontin is increased in the cerebrospinal fluid of patients with Alzheimer's disease and its levels correlate with cognitive decline.", annotationSubgraph:["Cytokine signaling subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebrospinal Fluid"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20308780", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20308780"}}, {start: {_id:2082}, end: {_id:2081}, properties:{evidence:"In MCI converters individuals tested longitudinally, both plasma and CSF OPN concentrations were significantly elevated when they received a diagnosis of AD during followup.", annotationSubgraph:["Chemokine signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Plasma", "Cerebrospinal Fluid"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"23576854", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23576854"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2081}, properties:{evidence:"In MCI converters individuals tested longitudinally, both plasma and CSF OPN concentrations were significantly elevated when they received a diagnosis of AD during followup.", annotationSubgraph:["Chemokine signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Plasma", "Cerebrospinal Fluid"], pmid:"23576854", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23576854"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2083}, properties:{evidence:"Neuronal expression of myeloperoxidase is increased in Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Myeloperoxidase subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"15255951", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15255951"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2081}, properties:{evidence:"Increased expression of the remodeling- and tumorigenic-associated factor osteopontin in pyramidal neurons of the Alzheimer's disease brain.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Neurons"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17316167", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17316167"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2205}, properties:{evidence:"A functional -463G/A MPO promoter polymorphism has been associated with AD risk through as yet unidentified mechanisms.", pmid:"19059911", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19059911"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2261}, properties:{evidence:"This pattern suggests an overall increase of COX-1 expression in AD. The present study shows that COX-1 is widely expressed in human brain, and raises the possibility that COX-1 may contribute to CNS pathology.", annotationSubgraph:["Prostaglandin subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"10560656", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10560656"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1726}, properties:{evidence:"We previously showed ECE-2 and ET-1 to be elevated in postmortem temporal cortex from AD patients, and ECE-2 expression and ET-1 release to be upregulated by Abeta42 in vitro.", annotationSubgraph:["Endothelin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebral Cortex"], pmid:"23629587", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23629587"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1359}, properties:{evidence:"We previously showed ECE-2 and ET-1 to be elevated in postmortem temporal cortex from AD patients, and ECE-2 expression and ET-1 release to be upregulated by Abeta42 in vitro.", annotationSubgraph:["Endothelin subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebral Cortex"], pmid:"23629587", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23629587"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1726}, properties:{evidence:"Western blot analysis indicates a significantly higher level of ET-1 in AD vessels compared to vessels from age-matched controls.", pmid:"20634595", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20634595"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1726}, properties:{evidence:"ET-1 and ECE-2 are also elevated in AD, making it likely that upregulation of the ECE-2-ET-1 axis by Abeta42 contributes to the chronic reduction of CBF in AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Endothelin subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21193044", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21193044"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2297}, properties:{evidence:"ET-1 and ECE-2 are also elevated in AD, making it likely that upregulation of the ECE-2-ET-1 axis by Abeta42 contributes to the chronic reduction of CBF in AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Endothelin subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21193044", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21193044"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1726}, properties:{evidence:"ET-1 mRNA measured in the temporal neocortex was significantly elevated in AD when normalized for expression of GAPDH (p = 0.0256) or the neuronal marker neuron-specific enolase (NSE, p = 0.0001). ET-1 protein was also significantly higher in AD than in control tissue, when adjusted for neuronal content by measurement of NSE (p = 0.0275).", annotationMeshanatomy:["Neocortex", "Neurons"], pmid:"22330820", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22330820"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2314}, properties:{evidence:"Our results show an upregulation of gene expression in AD patients for c-Fos and BAK.", annotationSubgraph:["Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], pmid:"17712163", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17712163"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1595}, properties:{evidence:"Our results show an upregulation of gene expression in AD patients for c-Fos and BAK.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], pmid:"17712163", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17712163"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:POSITIVE_CORRELATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1054}, properties:{evidence:"ICE-beta, c-Jun, Bax-alpha, Bcl-x(L), p53, and GADD153 were found to be upregulated in some AD samples but were not detected or downregulated in other AD or normal samples.", annotationSubgraph:["Caspase subgraph"], pmid:"17712163", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17712163"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2307}, properties:{evidence:"ICE-beta, c-Jun, Bax-alpha, Bcl-x(L), p53, and GADD153 were found to be upregulated in some AD samples but were not detected or downregulated in other AD or normal samples.", annotationSubgraph:["Response DNA damage"], pmid:"17712163", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17712163"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:561}, properties:{evidence:"ICE-beta, c-Jun, Bax-alpha, Bcl-x(L), p53, and GADD153 were found to be upregulated in some AD samples but were not detected or downregulated in other AD or normal samples.", annotationSubgraph:["p53 stabilization subgraph"], pmid:"17712163", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17712163"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1458}, properties:{evidence:"ICE-beta, c-Jun, Bax-alpha, Bcl-x(L), p53, and GADD153 were found to be upregulated in some AD samples but were not detected or downregulated in other AD or normal samples.", annotationSubgraph:["JAK-STAT signaling subgraph"], pmid:"17712163", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17712163"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2350}, properties:{evidence:"UPAR protein levels were significantly increased in human brain tissues from the hippocampus, superior frontal gyrus and inferior temporal gyrus of AD cases compared with similar tissues from non- demented cases.", annotationSubgraph:["Plasminogen activator subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Hippocampus"], pmid:"11814408", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11814408"}}, {start: {_id:434}, end: {_id:2421}, properties:{evidence:"Increased expression of phosphorylated S6, phosphorylated S6 kinase, phosphorylated eukaryotic initiation factor 4E binding protein 1, and phosphorylated mTOR was observed in DS hippocampus compared with controls.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Down Syndrome"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Hippocampus"], pmid:"24918639", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24918639"}}, {start: {_id:434}, end: {_id:2422}, properties:{evidence:"Increased expression of phosphorylated S6, phosphorylated S6 kinase, phosphorylated eukaryotic initiation factor 4E binding protein 1, and phosphorylated mTOR was observed in DS hippocampus compared with controls.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Down Syndrome"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Hippocampus"], pmid:"24918639", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24918639"}}, {start: {_id:434}, end: {_id:2423}, properties:{evidence:"Increased expression of phosphorylated S6, phosphorylated S6 kinase, phosphorylated eukaryotic initiation factor 4E binding protein 1, and phosphorylated mTOR was observed in DS hippocampus compared with controls.", annotationSubgraph:["mTOR signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Down Syndrome"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Hippocampus"], pmid:"24918639", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24918639"}}, {start: {_id:2424}, end: {_id:2425}, properties:{evidence:"Postpartum biopsies were collected in five weight-matched GDM women with IGT (GDM/IGT). GDM women had decreased skeletal muscle insulin- stimulated insulin receptor and insulin receptor substrate 1 (IRS1) tyrosine activation and reduced IRS1, concomitant with increased basal IRS1 serine phosphorylation and basal p70 S6-kinase (S6K1) activation, which resolved postpartum.", annotationSubgraph:["Interferon signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Diabetes, Gestational"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21289241", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21289241"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2431}, properties:{evidence:"The proinflammatory enzyme 5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX) is upregulated in Alzheimer's disease (AD), but its localization and association with the hallmark lesions of the disease, beta-amyloid (Abeta) plaques and neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs), is unknown.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Eicosanoids signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Plaque, Amyloid", "Alzheimer Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Neurofibrillary Tangles"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"18678882", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18678882"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:410}, properties:{evidence:"In this study, we demonstrated for the first time an increased CD44 gene expression in lymphocytes derived from Alzheimer's disease (AD) patients in comparison with healthy subjects.", annotationSubgraph:["Cell adhesion subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationCell:["lymphocyte"], pmid:"20197694", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20197694"}}, {start: {_id:2183}, end: {_id:1049}, properties:{evidence:"To examine possible caspase-9-activation in human sporadic ALS, we performed immunohistochemistry using anti-active caspase-9 antibody on post mortem human samples. Four of the eight ALS spinal cords showed obvious caspase-9 activation in the motor neurons studied, but this was not seen in any of the controls (Figure 5A); this suggests that caspase-9 may play an instrumental role in some forms of human sporadic ALS", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Motor Neurons"], pmid:"14657037", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"14657037"}}, {start: {_id:2183}, end: {_id:1678}, properties:{evidence:"In contrast, in symptomatic mice, expression of Bcl-2 and Bcl-XL, which inhibit apoptotic process, is reduced, whereas expression of Bad and Bax, which stimulate apoptotic process, is increased. These alterations are specific to affected brain regions and are caused by the mutant and not by the normal SOD1 enzyme.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Spinal Cord"], pmid:"10582606", annotationDisease:["amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10582606"}}, {start: {_id:2183}, end: {_id:2529}, properties:{evidence:"In contrast, in symptomatic mice, expression of Bcl-2 and Bcl-XL, which inhibit apoptotic process, is reduced, whereas expression of Bad and Bax, which stimulate apoptotic process, is increased. These alterations are specific to affected brain regions and are caused by the mutant and not by the normal SOD1 enzyme.", annotationSubgraph:["Electron transport chain", "Caspase subgraph", "Bcl-2 subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Spinal Cord"], pmid:"10582606", annotationDisease:["amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10582606"}}, {start: {_id:733}, end: {_id:6}, properties:{evidence:"Insulin not only regulates blood sugar concentrations but also acts as a growth factor on all cell including neurons in the central nervous system [38]. Brain resistance to insulin/IGF-I accounts for neuronal atrophy and death, tangle formation and brain amyloidosis typical of AD pathology [47].", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"23557940", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23557940"}}, {start: {_id:4071}, end: {_id:837}, properties:{evidence:"An important factor in the onset of inflammatory process is the overexpression of interleukin (IL)-1, which produces many reactions in a vicious circle that cause dysfunction and neuronal death. Other important cytokines in neuroinflammation are IL-6 and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-Î±. By contrast, other cytokines such as IL-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1ra), IL-4, IL-10, and transforming growth factor (TGF)-beta can suppress both proinflammatory cytokine production and their action, subsequently protecting the brain.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22566778", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22566778"}}, {start: {_id:4071}, end: {_id:746}, properties:{evidence:"An important factor in the onset of inflammatory process is the overexpression of interleukin (IL)-1, which produces many reactions in a vicious circle that cause dysfunction and neuronal death. Other important cytokines in neuroinflammation are IL-6 and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-Î±. By contrast, other cytokines such as IL-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1ra), IL-4, IL-10, and transforming growth factor (TGF)-beta can suppress both proinflammatory cytokine production and their action, subsequently protecting the brain.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Interleukin signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22566778", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22566778"}}, {start: {_id:4071}, end: {_id:96}, properties:{evidence:"An important factor in the onset of inflammatory process is the overexpression of interleukin (IL)-1, which produces many reactions in a vicious circle that cause dysfunction and neuronal death. Other important cytokines in neuroinflammation are IL-6 and tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-Î±. By contrast, other cytokines such as IL-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1ra), IL-4, IL-10, and transforming growth factor (TGF)-beta can suppress both proinflammatory cytokine production and their action, subsequently protecting the brain.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22566778", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22566778"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:1561}, properties:{evidence:"Here, we aim to summarize recent studies that suggest the potential correlation between adiponectin and Alzheimer's disease.", annotationConfidence:["Low"], pmid:"24386594", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24386594"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:4100}, properties:{evidence:"Microglia manage immunosurveillance and mediate inflammation, both suggested to be important in Alzheimer's disease (AD). The aim of this study was to investigate if microglial markers could differentiate, firstly between AD and controls, and secondly between stable mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and those progressing to AD and vascular dementia (VaD). Furthermore, we investigated if these markers were sufficiently stable to be used in clinical trials. We quantified YKL-40 and sCD14 in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from 96 AD patients, 65 healthy controls, and 170 patients with MCI from baseline and over 5.7 years. For the stability analysis, two CSF samples were collected from 52 AD patients with a six-month interval in between. YKL-40, but not sCD14, was significantly elevated in AD compared with healthy controls (p = 0.003). Furthermore, YKL-40 and sCD14 were increased in MCI patients who converted to VaD (p = 0.029 and p = 0.008), but not to AD according to NINCDS-ADRDA. However, when stratified according to CSF levels of tau and ABeta42, YKL-40 was elevated in those with an AD- indicative profile compared with stable MCI with a normal profile (p = 0.037). In addition, YKL-40 and sCD14 were very stable in AD patients with good correlation between time-points (r = 0.94, p = 3.4 x 10-25; r = 0.77, p = 2.0 x 10-11) and the cortical damage marker T-tau. Thus, microglial markers are stable and may be used as safety markers for monitoring CNS inflammation and microglia activation in clinical trials. Moreover, YKL-40 differentiates between AD and controls and between stable MCI to AD and those that convert to AD and VaD.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22890100", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22890100"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:POSITIVE_CORRELATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:561}, properties:{evidence:"the p53 protein levels in each stage of AD development remained statistically nonsignificantly higher compared to the controls", annotationSubgraph:["p53 stabilization subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21845541", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21845541"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:3236}, properties:{evidence:"Nevertheless, the CSF level of neurogranin is selectively increased in AD dementia", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebrospinal Fluid"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"26783546", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26783546"}}, {start: {_id:825}, end: {_id:4058}, properties:{evidence:"Intriguingly, while expression of Dkk1 is required for proper neural development, overexpression of Dkk1 is characteristic of many neurodegenerative diseases, such as stroke, Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease, and temporal lobe epilepsy.", annotationSubgraph:["DKK1 subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Hippocampus"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"23261660", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23261660"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:2750}, properties:{evidence:"Accumulation of the amyloid beta (Abeta) peptide derived from the amyloid precursor protein (APP) plays a central role in the pathogenesis of Alzheimer's disease (AD). We previously reported that the scaffolding protein RanBP9 is markedly increased in AD brains and promotes Abeta generation by scaffolding APP/BACE1/LRP complexes together and accelerating APP endocytosis.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22223749", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22223749"}}, {start: {_id:27}, end: {_id:4957}, properties:{evidence:"Levels of TIMP-1 were significantly elevated in CSF samples from all disease groups. TIMP-2 was significantly increased in CSF of AD and HD patients. MMP-2 levels did not differ significantly between groups. These findings show that TIMPs are elevated in the CSF of patients with neurodegenerative diseases suggesting a potential role of these endogenous inhibitors of matrix metalloproteinases in neurodegenerative diseases.", annotationSubgraph:["Matrix metalloproteinase subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Cerebrospinal Fluid"], pmid:"12614934", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:" 12614934"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:POSITIVE_CORRELATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:151}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"gene mutation of presenilin, which is a component of the gamma- secretase complex and is associated with familial AD, results in an attenuation of ER chaperone expression during ER stress. Mutations in presenilin inhibit activation of IRE1, ATF6, and PERK, all of which act as signal transducers of ER stress in the ER membrane (for details, see the article by Hosoi and Ozawa in this Forum Minireview series: Ref. 14), thereby making neurons vulnerable to ER stress. In addition, nitric oxideâ€“induced S-nitrosylation of ER chaperone protein disulfide isomerase (PDI) inhibits its enzymatic activity, leading to ER stressâ€“induced neuronal death. Interestingly, S-nitrosylation of PDI has been observed in the brain of sporadic AD and PD patients. ER stress can also cause AD onset. For example, eIF2a phosphorylation, which is induced by eIF2a kinase PERK, elevates BACE1 (Abeta-secretase) levels and causes increased Abeta production (17). Furthermore, Abeta has been reported to induce ER stress, leading to activation of ER stressâ€“specific initiator caspases, including mouse caspase- 12 and human caspase-4. In conclusion, because postmortem AD patient brain samples exhibit activated UPR markers, including phosphorylated PERK, eIF2a, and IRE1, ER stress probably plays a key role in AD pathogenesis as a cause or consequence", annotationSubgraph:["Gamma secretase subgraph", "Unfolded protein response subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22382662", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22382662"}}, {start: {_id:612}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"BACE1 is primarily expressed by neurons and increased BACE1 protein concentrations and enzymatic activities have been reported in the brains of AD patients. However, there is accumulating evidence that, in addition to neurons, reactive astrocytes are capable of expressing BACE1 and, therefore, may contribute to beta-amyloid plaque formation. This suggests that conditions accompanied by chronic astrocyte activation may contribute to developing AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"15465276", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15465276"}}, {start: {_id:89}, end: {_id:825}, properties:{evidence:"Microglia are activated in response to a number of different pathological states within the CNS including injury, ischemia, and infection. Microglial activation results in their production of pro- inflammatory cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-a. While release of these factors is typically intended to prevent further damage to CNS tissue, they may also be toxic to neurons and other glial cells. Mounting evidence indicates that chronic microglial activation may also contribute to the development and progression of neurodegenerative disorders.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22024597", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22024597"}}, {start: {_id:826}, end: {_id:92}, properties:{evidence:"Chronic neuroinflammation is a hallmark of several neurological disorders associated with cognitive loss. Activated microglia and secreted factors such as tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-a are key mediators of neuroinflammation and may contribute to neuronal dysfunction", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22277195", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22277195"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:92}, properties:{evidence:"Chronic neuroinflammation is a hallmark of several neurological disorders associated with cognitive loss. Activated microglia and secreted factors such as tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-a are key mediators of neuroinflammation and may contribute to neuronal dysfunction", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22277195", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22277195"}}, {start: {_id:91}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"Recently, there have been increasing evidences that microRNA-146 (miR-146) is related to up-regulated immune and inflammatory signaling through its target genes, such as IRAK1 and TRAF6. Additionally, abundant data continue to support the hypothesis that progressive up- regulation of inflammatory gene expression and elevated inflammatory signaling facilitate the development and progression of Alzheimer's disease (AD). This review focuses on the recent findings regarding the role of miR-146 in modulating immune response and its subsequent effects in the pathogenesis of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "miRNA subgraph", "Toll like receptor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22209051", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22209051"}}, {start: {_id:151}, end: {_id:1142}, properties:{evidence:"ER stress contributes to the pathogenesis of obesity and diabetes, which are risk factors for Alzheimer's disease (AD) that accelerate the pathogenesis of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction", "Unfolded protein response subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22115781", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22115781"}}, {start: {_id:151}, end: {_id:1098}, properties:{evidence:"ER stress contributes to the pathogenesis of obesity and diabetes, which are risk factors for Alzheimer's disease (AD) that accelerate the pathogenesis of AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Insulin signal transduction", "Unfolded protein response subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22115781", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22115781"}}, {start: {_id:612}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"This suggests that conditions accompanied by chronic astrocyte activation may contribute to developing AD.", annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"15465276", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15465276"}}, {start: {_id:4019}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"CEBPD is upregulated in the astrocytes of AD patients. Therefore, we asked if activation of astrocytic/ CEBPD could contribute to AD pathogenesis. In this report, a novel role of CEBPD in attenuating macrophage-mediated/ phagocytosis of damaged neuron cells was found. By global gene expression profiling, we identified the inflammatory/ marker pentraxin-3 (PTX3, TNFAIP5, TSG-14) as a CEBPD target in astrocytes. Furthermore, we demonstrate that PTX3/ participates in the attenuation of macrophage-mediated phagocytosis of damaged neuron cells. This study provides the/ first demonstration of a role for astrocytic CEBPD and the CEBPD-regulated molecule PTX3 in the accumulation of damaged/ neurons, which is a hallmark of AD pathogenesis.", annotationSubgraph:["Innate immune system subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Astrocytes"], pmid:"21112127", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21112127"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:4071}, properties:{evidence:"AGEs are also known to activate glia, resulting in inflammation and neuronal dysfunction. As reactive intermediates of AGE formation, neurotoxic reactive dicarbonyl compounds such as glyoxal and methylglyoxal have been identified. One of the most effective detoxification systems for methylglyoxal and glyoxal is the glutathione-dependent glyoxalase system, consisting of glyoxalase I and glyoxalase II. In this study, we have determined the methylglyoxal and glyoxal levels in the cerebrospinal fluid of AD patients compared to healthy controls. Methylglyoxal levels in AD patients were twofold higher than in controls, but this difference was not significant due to the large intergroup variations and the small sample size. However, the concentrations of both compounds were five to seven times higher in CSF than in plasma. We also investigated the glyoxalase I level in AD and healthy control brains. The number of glyoxalase I- positive neurons were increased in AD brains compared to controls. Our findings suggest that glyoxalase I is upregulated in AD in a compensatory manner to maintain physiological methylglyoxal and glyoxal levels.", annotationSubgraph:["Immunoglobulin subgraph"], pmid:"16037241", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16037241"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"Glial activation and increased inflammation characterize neuropathology in Alzheimer's disease (AD). The aim was to develop a model for studying phagocytosis of beta-amyloid (Abeta) peptide by human microglia and to test effects thereupon by immunomodulatory substances. Human CHME3 microglia showed intracellular Abeta(1-42) colocalized with lysosome-associated membrane protein-2, indicating phagocytosis. This was increased by interferon-gamma, and to a lesser degree with Protollin, a proteosome-based adjuvant. Secretion of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) was decreased by Abeta(1-42) and by interferon-gamma and interleukin-1beta. These cytokines, but not Abeta(1-42), stimulated interleukin-6 release. Microglia which phagocytosed Abeta(1-42) exhibited a higher degree of expression of interleukin-1 receptor type I and inducible nitric oxide synthase. In conclusion, we show that human microglia are able to phagocytose Abeta(1-42) and that this is associated with expression of inflammatory markers. Abeta(1-42) and interferon-gamma decreased BDNF secretion suggesting a new neuropathological role for Abeta(1-42) and the inflammation accompanying AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Chaperone subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Microglia"], pmid:"20798889", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20798889"}}, {start: {_id:270}, end: {_id:2025}, properties:{evidence:"Collectively, Evo induced an influx of extracellular calcium, which led to JNK-mediated protective autophagy, and this provides a new option for ischemic stroke treatment.", annotationSubgraph:["Autophagy signaling subgraph"], annotationMeshdisease:["Stroke"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"24454492", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24454492"}}, {start: {_id:91}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"Our study demonstrated that TLR2 is a primary receptor for Abeta to trigger neuroinflammatory activation and suggested that inhibition of TLR2 in microglia could be beneficial in Alzheimer's disease pathogenesis.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Toll like receptor subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Microglia"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22198949", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], annotationCellline:["HEK293", "RAW264.7"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22198949"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"Microglia are resident mononuclear phagocytes of the brain that become activated in response to insults including neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease and prion disease.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Neurodegenerative Diseases", "Parkinson Disease", "Prion Diseases", "Alzheimer Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Microglia", "Phagocytes"], pmid:"24655482", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24655482"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:825}, properties:{evidence:"Microglia are resident mononuclear phagocytes of the brain that become activated in response to insults including neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease and prion disease.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Neurodegenerative Diseases", "Parkinson Disease", "Prion Diseases", "Alzheimer Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Microglia", "Phagocytes"], pmid:"24655482", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24655482"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:195}, properties:{evidence:"Microglia are resident mononuclear phagocytes of the brain that become activated in response to insults including neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease and prion disease.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Neurodegenerative Diseases", "Parkinson Disease", "Prion Diseases", "Alzheimer Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Microglia", "Phagocytes"], pmid:"24655482", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24655482"}}, {start: {_id:721}, end: {_id:4342}, properties:{evidence:"Microglia are resident mononuclear phagocytes of the brain that become activated in response to insults including neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease and prion disease.", annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationMeshdisease:["Neurodegenerative Diseases", "Parkinson Disease", "Prion Diseases", "Alzheimer Disease"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain", "Microglia", "Phagocytes"], pmid:"24655482", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24655482"}}, {start: {_id:4434}, end: {_id:4071}, properties:{evidence:"JAK-STAT signaling as an anti-inflammatory target. JAK-STAT signaling mediates the brain inflammation induced by LPS, IFN-gamma, ganglioside and thrombin. Curcumin activates SH2-containing phosphatase 2 (SHP2), while rosiglitazone and 15d-PGJ2 increase the expressions of SOCS1 and SOCS3. SHP2 and the SOCS proteins are typical negative feedback molecules of the JAK-STAT pathway.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "JAK-STAT signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"26113788", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"26113788"}}, {start: {_id:4322}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"suppressed IGFBP3 expression due to Abeta-induced epigenetic modifications could be vulnerable to Abeta toxicity, and chronic exposure to Abeta protein could lead to damage or death in AD-related brain regions.we demonstrate for the first time in AD an anti- apoptotic role of IGFBP3 that protects brain cells from toxic Abeta1ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Å“42 as well as IGFBP3 modification by DNA methylation-dependent regulation.", annotationSpecies:["10090"], annotationUserdefinedcellline:["App transgenic"], pmid:"24964199", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24964199", annotationRace:["Swedish"]}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:4322}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"Calpains are involved in calcium-induced neuronal cell toxicity, which is associated with the pathophysiology of Alzheimer's disease (AD)", pmid:"24200051", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24200051"}}, {start: {_id:217}, end: {_id:27}, properties:{evidence:"Mn neurotoxicity is also known to contribute to the development of multiple neurodegenerative disorders including AD,", pmid:"25064045", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"25064045"}}, {start: {_id:70}, end: {_id:825}, properties:{evidence:"Neurons in the brain are considered to be particularly vulnerable to oxidative stress, leading to neuronal oxidative damage and neurodegenerative disorders such as Alzheimer's disease (AD) and senile dementia. The process of fusing synaptic plasma membranes and synaptic vesicles involves particular proteins, such as the soluble NSF (N-ethylmaleimide-sensitive factor) attachment protein receptor (SNARE) proteins for docking both membranes, and is integral to neurotransmission. To elucidate whether oxidative stress induces denaturation of SNARE proteins, and whether vitamin E can counteract this process, changes in the expression of synaptobrevin, synaptotagmin, SNAP-25, and syntaxin-1 in rat brain nerve terminals were analyzed using an immunoblotting method. The results showed that oxidative stress induced significant reductions in the levels synaptobrevin and synaptotagmin in synaptic vesicles. Similarly, marked decreases in the levels of SNAP-25 and syntaxin-1 in pre- synaptic plasma membranes were also observed. In the absence of oxidative stress, vitamin E-deficient rats exhibited similar decreases in these proteins.", annotationSubgraph:["Synaptic vesicle endocytosis subgraph", "Response to oxidative stress"], pmid:"21971407", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21971407"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:112}, properties:{evidence:"Application of the amyloid beta-peptide A beta(1-42) induces the conversion of p35 to p25 in primary cortical neurons", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "Cyclin-CDK subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Neurons"], annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"10830966", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10830966"}}, {start: {_id:285}, end: {_id:1105}, properties:{evidence:"Although the requirements for binding of Fe65 and X11 to the GYENPTY motif differ slightly, they are sufficiently overlapping to make it conceivable that these proteins compete for binding to APP. Interestingly, opposite effects on APP processing have been observed after co-expression of APP with either Fe65 or X11. Fe65 overexpression increased the amount of cell-surface-associated APP, as well as the processing of APP to APPs and Ab peptide. By contrast, X11 decreased processing and cellular retention of APP. Thus, the functional balance between the two proteins is important for regulation of APP metabolism and possibly APP function. This balance could be further modified by a third protein, mDAB1 (the mammalian homologue of Disabled), that binds to the same region", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"10806097", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"10806097"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:4385}, end: {_id:1935}, properties:{evidence:"Knockdown of miR-181 enhanced LPS-induced production of pro- inflammatory cytokines (TNF-Î±, IL-6, IL-1beta, / IL-8) and HMGB1, while overexpression of miR-181 resulted in a significant increase in the expression of the anti-inflamma/ tory cytokine IL-10. To assess the effects of miR-181 on the astrocyte transcriptome, we performed gene array and pathway / analysis on astrocytes with reduced levels of miR-181b/c. To examine the pool of potential miR-181 targets, we employed / a biotin pull-down of miR-181c and gene array analysis. We validated the mRNAs encoding MeCP2 and X-linked inhibitor of / apoptotic process as targets of miR-181. These findings suggest that miR-181 plays important roles in the molecular responses of / astrocytes in inflammatory settings.", annotationSubgraph:["miRNA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"23650073", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"23650073"}}] AS row
