nce:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1795}, end: {_id:1776}, properties:{evidence:"Galantamine increases the availability of ACh in the cholinergic synapse by competitively inhibiting AChE, the enzyme responsible for its breakdown. Galantamine also potentiates cholinergic neurotransmission by positively modulating the response of nAChRs to ACh.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1795}, end: {_id:1778}, properties:{evidence:"Galantamine increases the availability of ACh in the cholinergic synapse by competitively inhibiting AChE, the enzyme responsible for its breakdown. Galantamine also potentiates cholinergic neurotransmission by positively modulating the response of nAChRs to ACh.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1795}, end: {_id:1780}, properties:{evidence:"Galantamine increases the availability of ACh in the cholinergic synapse by competitively inhibiting AChE, the enzyme responsible for its breakdown. Galantamine also potentiates cholinergic neurotransmission by positively modulating the response of nAChRs to ACh.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1795}, end: {_id:1782}, properties:{evidence:"Galantamine increases the availability of ACh in the cholinergic synapse by competitively inhibiting AChE, the enzyme responsible for its breakdown. Galantamine also potentiates cholinergic neurotransmission by positively modulating the response of nAChRs to ACh.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1795}, end: {_id:1784}, properties:{evidence:"Galantamine increases the availability of ACh in the cholinergic synapse by competitively inhibiting AChE, the enzyme responsible for its breakdown. Galantamine also potentiates cholinergic neurotransmission by positively modulating the response of nAChRs to ACh.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1795}, end: {_id:1786}, properties:{evidence:"Galantamine increases the availability of ACh in the cholinergic synapse by competitively inhibiting AChE, the enzyme responsible for its breakdown. Galantamine also potentiates cholinergic neurotransmission by positively modulating the response of nAChRs to ACh.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11230880", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11230880"}}, {start: {_id:1841}, end: {_id:1848}, properties:{evidence:"We found a significant reduction of calyculin A-induced tau hyperphosphorylation at Ser198/199/202, Ser396, Ser404, Thr205, and Thr231 24 h after treatment with 20 Î¼g/ml berberine. Berberine also restored protein phosphates 2A activity and reversed glycogen synthase kinase-3beta (GSK-3beta) activation, as determined by phosphatase activity assay and GSK-3beta phosphorylation at Tyr216 and Ser9, respectively. Furthermore, berberine reversed both the increase of malondialdehyde and the decrease of superoxide dismutase activity induced by calyculin A, indicating its role in anti-oxidative stress.", annotationSubgraph:["Free radical formation subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"21297267", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21297267"}}, {start: {_id:1866}, end: {_id:1868}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:INCREASES]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:1854}, end: {_id:1868}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1866}, end: {_id:1870}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1866}, end: {_id:1872}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1859}, end: {_id:1870}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1859}, end: {_id:1872}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1854}, end: {_id:1870}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1854}, end: {_id:1872}, properties:{evidence:"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is regulated by cholinergic mechanisms. Acetylcholine (ACh), the cholinergic neurotransmitter, has been reported to alter the levels of cortisol as well as those of prolactin, growth hormone, leutinizing hormone and vasopressin. Prolactin secretion, however, is suppressed by increased pure cholinergic activity. Central actions of cholinomimetic drugs that either increase the concentration of ACh in brain (e.g. physostigmine) or activate the postsynaptic muscarinic receptors (e.g. arecoline), can also activate the HPA axis in both animals and human subjects.", annotationMeshanatomy:["Brain"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1866}, end: {_id:1875}, properties:{evidence:"Cumulative evidence from human and animal studies suggests that central activation of the HPA axis by cholinergic drugs can result in the elevation of plasma levels of several neuropeptides including adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH), cortisol, b-endorphin, vasopressin, and epinephrine. Neuropeptides can modulate neurotransmission and can effect cognition. It has, therefore, been speculated that cognitive enhancement by cholinergic drugs could be mediated by the central activation of the HPA axis.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"8584603", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"8584603"}}, {start: {_id:1826}, end: {_id:797}, properties:{evidence:"Etazolate is a phosphodiesterase 4 (PDE4) inhibitor and GABAA receptor modulator that also stimulates alpha-secretase activity and neurotrophic soluble amyloid precursor protein (sAPPalpha) production, currently developed as a possible Alzheimer's disease therapeutic.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["10116"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20223232", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20223232"}}, {start: {_id:1826}, end: {_id:797}, properties:{evidence:"EHT0202 (etazolate hydrochloride) is a new compound exhibiting both potential disease-modifying and symptomatic treatment properties in Alzheimer's Disease increasing alpha-secretase activity and sAPP alpha secretion, as well as acting as a GABA-A receptor modulator and as a PDE-4 inhibitor.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationSpecies:["9606"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21222604", annotationFdastatus:["Phase 2"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21222604"}}, {start: {_id:1898}, end: {_id:1905}, properties:{evidence:"We have previously demonstrated that the nootropic drug nefiracetam potentiates the activity of both nicotinic acetylcholine and NMDA receptors and that nefiracetam modulates the glycine binding site of the NMDA receptor.", annotationSubgraph:["Acetylcholine signaling subgraph", "GABA subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17095583", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17095583"}}, {start: {_id:1898}, end: {_id:1766}, properties:{eviden