DA receptors. Enhanced synaptic activity drives APP processing via the amyloidogenic ÃƒÅ¸ -secretase pathway, leading to subsequent spine loss and downregulation of glutamate receptors in a negative feedback loop.", annotationSubgraph:["Synapse assembly subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21199446", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21199446"}}, {start: {_id:462}, end: {_id:903}, properties:{evidence:"APP is expressed pre- and postsynaptically and promotes synapse formation via trans-synaptic interactions of its extracellular domains. Full-length APP also may promote dendritic spine formation as well as surface expression of GluA2-containing AMPA receptors and GluN2B-containing NMDA receptors. Enhanced synaptic activity drives APP processing via the amyloidogenic ÃƒÅ¸ -secretase pathway, leading to subsequent spine loss and downregulation of glutamate receptors in a negative feedback loop.", annotationSubgraph:["Synapse assembly subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"21199446", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21199446"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:919}, properties:{evidence:"Consistent with our cell biological studies implicating APP in regulation of dendritic spines and NMDAR trafficking, numerous behavioral studies suggest that APP influences synaptic plasticity as well as learning and memory.", pmid:"21199446", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21199446"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:668}, properties:{evidence:"Consistent with our cell biological studies implicating APP in regulation of dendritic spines and NMDAR trafficking, numerous behavioral studies suggest that APP influences synaptic plasticity as well as learning and memory.", pmid:"21199446", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21199446"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:193}, properties:{evidence:"Consistent with our cell biological studies implicating APP in regulation of dendritic spines and NMDAR trafficking, numerous behavioral studies suggest that APP influences synaptic plasticity as well as learning and memory.", pmid:"21199446", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"21199446"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:3884}, properties:{evidence:"APPs is constitutively released from cells following a-secretase cleavage, these findings indicated that APP has autocrine and paracrine functions in growth regulation.", annotationSubgraph:["Cytokine signaling subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"18650430", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"18650430"}}] AS row
