UNWIND [{start: {_id:30}, end: {_id:32}, properties:{evidence:"Calsyntenin-1 is a ligand for kinesin-1 light chains and APP is transported through axons on kinesin-1 molecular motors. Defects in axonal transport are an early pathological feature in Alzheimer's disease and defective APP transport is known to increase Abeta production. We show that calsyntenin-1 and APP are co-transported through axons and that siRNA-induced loss of calsyntenin-1 markedly disrupts axonal transport of APP. Thus, perturbation to axonal transport of APP on calsyntenin-1 containing carriers induces alterations to APP processing that increase production of Abeta. Together, our findings suggest that disruption of calsyntenin-1-associated axonal transport of APP is a pathogenic mechanism in Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Axonal transport subgraph", "Calsyntenin subgraph"], annotationTextlocation:["Abstract"], annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"15491160", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15491160"}}, {start: {_id:28}, end: {_id:32}, properties:{evidence:"Calsyntenin-1 is a ligand for kinesin-1 light chains and APP is transported through axons on kinesin-1 molecular motors. Defects in axonal transport are an early pathological feature in Alzheimer's disease and defective APP transport is known to increase Abeta production. We show that calsyntenin-1 and APP are co-transported through axons and that siRNA-induced loss of calsyntenin-1 markedly disrupts axonal transport of APP. Thus, perturbation to axonal transport of APP on calsyntenin-1 containing carriers induces alterations to APP processing that increase production of Abeta. Together, our findings suggest that disruption of calsyntenin-1-associated axonal transport of APP is a pathogenic mechanism in Alzheimer's disease.", annotationSubgraph:["Non-amyloidogenic subgraph", "Axonal transport subgraph", "Calsyntenin subgraph"], annotationTextlocation:["Abstract"], annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"15491160", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15491160"}}, {start: {_id:174}, end: {_id:176}, properties:{evidence:"in addition to lipid transport mediated by apoE, cholesterol homeostasis in the brain is markedly altered in response to changes in the HMGR pathway; suggesting a possible explanation for the potentially beneficial effect of statins in common AD.", annotationSubgraph:["Cholesterol metabolism subgraph", "Lipid metabolism subgraph", "APOE subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Very High"], pmid:"15639314", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15639314"}}, {start: {_id:37}, end: {_id:215}, properties:{evidence:"PS1 also modulates basal level of ERK1/2 activity through a ras-Raf- MEK-dependent pathway activated by a direct binding with the SH2 domain of Grb2. ERK family is one of the most ubiquitous cellular signaling mechanisms, whose activation links extracellular stimuli to cell proliferation, survival, and differentiation, but also cell death and apoptotic process. In this respect, it is worth of note to observe, as mentioned above, that ERK1/2 pathway is also modulated by AbetaPP", annotationSubgraph:["Gamma secretase subgraph", "MAPK-ERK subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"22496686", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22496686"}}, {start: {_id:586}, end: {_id:577}, properties:{evidence:"The interaction of APP with APP-BP1 activates a pathway leading to the conjugation of NEDD8, a ubiquitin-like protein, to its target (Fig. 1, ref. 44). APP-BP1, together with hUba3, is functionally analogous to the ubiquitin activating enzyme E1, with hUba3 containing the active cysteine and ATP binding site. When NEDD8 is activated by the APP- BP1/hUba3 complex, it forms a thiol ester bond with hUbc12, which has a function parallel to that of ubiquitin-conjugating enzyme Cdc32. Subsequently, NEDD8 is covalently coupled to lysine residues in its target proteins (46). So far, the proteins known to be neddylated via this pathway are a family of proteins called cullins (47) and the Mdm2 oncogene product, which in turn regulates neddylation of the cell cycle protein p53. Cullins are scaffold proteins for the E3 ubiquitin ligase complex, and neddylation of cullin enhances its ability to promote ubiquitination (49,50). Indeed, NEDD8 has been found in ubiquitinated neurofibrillary tangles in AD brain (51). NEDD8 signalling has been shown to regulate protein degradation pathways participating in cell cycle progression (52â€“55). The discovery of a novel protein, NUB1, which recruits NEDD8-conjugates to the proteasome for degradation, provides a direct link between these 2 systems (56,57). Inhibition of the neddylation pathway in neurons by expression of a dominant negative mutant of hUbc12 prevents FAD APP- mediated cell cycle entry and apoptotic process (44,45). Thus, elements of this pathway are attractive targets for potential therapies aimed at preventing neurons in AD brain from entering the cell cycle.", annotationSubgraph:["Ubiquitin degradation subgraph"], pmid:"17113271", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17113271"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:1478}, properties:{evidence:"Among the different APP-interacting proteins, we focused our interest on the GRB2 adaptor protein, which connects cell surface receptors to intracellular signaling pathways. In this study we provide evidence by co-immunoprecipitation experiments, confocal and electron microscopy, and by fluorescence resonance energy transfer experiments that both APP and presenilin1 interact with GRB2 in vesicular structures at the centrosome of the cell. The final target for these interactions is ERK1,2, which is activated in mitotic centrosomes in a PS1- and APP- dependent manner. These data suggest that both APP and presenilin1 can be part of a common signaling pathway that regulates ERK1,2 and the cell cycle.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "MAPK-ERK subgraph", "Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17314098", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17314098"}}, {start: {_id:22}, end: {_id:1479}, properties:{evidence:"Among the different APP-interacting proteins, we focused our interest on the GRB2 adaptor protein, which connects cell surface receptors to intracellular signaling pathways. In this study we provide evidence by co-immunoprecipitation experiments, confocal and electron microscopy, and by fluorescence resonance energy transfer experiments that both APP and presenilin1 interact with GRB2 in vesicular structures at the centrosome of the cell. The final target for these interactions is ERK1,2, which is activated in mitotic centrosomes in a PS1- and APP- dependent manner. These data suggest that both APP and presenilin1 can be part of a common signaling pathway that regulates ERK1,2 and the cell cycle.", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph", "MAPK-ERK subgraph", "Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17314098", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17314098"}}, {start: {_id:37}, end: {_id:1478}, properties:{evidence:"Among the different APP-interacting proteins, we focused our interest on the GRB2 adaptor protein, which connects cell surface receptors to intracellular signaling pathways. In this study we provide evidence by co-immunoprecipitation experiments, confocal and electron microscopy, and by fluorescence resonance energy transfer experiments that both APP and presenilin1 interact with GRB2 in vesicular structures at the centrosome of the cell. The final target for these interactions is ERK1,2, which is activated in mitotic centrosomes in a PS1- and APP- dependent manner. These data suggest that both APP and presenilin1 can be part of a common signaling pathway that regulates ERK1,2 and the cell cycle.", annotationSubgraph:["Gamma secretase subgraph", "MAPK-ERK subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17314098", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17314098"}}, {start: {_id:37}, end: {_id:1479}, properties:{evidence:"Among the different APP-interacting proteins, we focused our interest on the GRB2 adaptor protein, which connects cell surface receptors to intracellular signaling pathways. In this study we provide evidence by co-immunoprecipitation experiments, confocal and electron microscopy, and by fluorescence resonance energy transfer experiments that both APP and presenilin1 interact with GRB2 in vesicular structures at the centrosome of the cell. The final target for these interactions is ERK1,2, which is activated in mitotic centrosomes in a PS1- and APP- dependent manner. These data suggest that both APP and presenilin1 can be part of a common signaling pathway that regulates ERK1,2 and the cell cycle.", annotationSubgraph:["Gamma secretase subgraph", "MAPK-ERK subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"17314098", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17314098"}}, {start: {_id:210}, end: {_id:1947}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, Pin1 cooperates with either activated Ras or JNK to increase transcriptional activity of c-Jun towards the cyclin D1 promoter.", annotationMeshdisease:["Neoplasms"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11432833", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11432833"}}, {start: {_id:342}, end: {_id:1947}, properties:{evidence:"Moreover, Pin1 cooperates with either activated Ras or JNK to increase transcriptional activity of c-Jun towards the cyclin D1 promoter.", annotationMeshdisease:["Neoplasms"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"11432833", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"11432833"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:206}, properties:{evidence:"Concurrently, Abeta alters erythrocyte cell morphology, decreases nitrites and nitrates levels, and affects membrane acetylcholinesterase activity.", annotationSubgraph:["Nitric oxide subgraph"], annotationCell:["erythrocyte"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"22431227", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"22431227"}}, {start: {_id:1926}, end: {_id:2187}, properties:{evidence:"New findings have also linked activation of the NRF2 system to anti- inflammatory effects via interactions with NF-ÎºB.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24262633", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24262633"}}, {start: {_id:1407}, end: {_id:2187}, properties:{evidence:"New findings have also linked activation of the NRF2 system to anti- inflammatory effects via interactions with NF-ÎºB.", annotationSubgraph:["Inflammatory response subgraph", "Tumor necrosis factor subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"24262633", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"24262633"}}, {start: {_id:2398}, end: {_id:1868}, properties:{evidence:"We previously showed that some familial Alzheimer's disease PS mutations cause increased basal and acetylcholine muscarinic receptor- stimulated phospholipase C (PLC) activity which was gamma-secretase dependent.", annotationSubgraph:["Phosphatidylinositol 3 subgraph"], pmid:"17437536", annotationDisease:["Alzheimer's disease"], citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17437536"}}, {start: {_id:2739}, end: {_id:21}, properties:{evidence:"These results suggest that PAR-4 may be directly involved in regulating the APP cleavage activity of BACE1.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"15671026", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"15671026"}}, {start: {_id:1926}, end: {_id:21}, properties:{evidence:"In addition, we show that some NF-kappaB inhibitors decrease sAPPbeta and APP-CTFbeta suggesting that they reduce the beta-secretase cleavage of APP.", annotationSubgraph:["Nuclear factor Kappa beta subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"17223266", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"17223266"}}, {start: {_id:2647}, end: {_id:21}, properties:{evidence:"Because beta-site APP-cleaving enzyme 1 (BACE1), an essential protease for Abeta production, is up-regulated in cells overexpressing RAGE and in RAGE-injected brains of Tg2576 mice, the molecular mechanisms underlying RAGE, BACE1 expression, and Abeta production were examined.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"19332646", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"19332646"}}, {start: {_id:1420}, end: {_id:21}, properties:{evidence:"Taken together, ATXN1 functions as a genetic risk pmodifier that contributes to AD pathogenesis through a loss-of-function mechanism by regulating beta-secretase cleavage of APP and Abeta levels.", annotationSubgraph:["Beta secretase subgraph", "Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20097758", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20097758"}}, {start: {_id:3056}, end: {_id:1184}, properties:{evidence:"A synthetic peptide, S3 peptide, which acts as a specific competitor for ADF/cofilin phosphorylation by LIMK1, inhibited fAbeta-induced ADF/cofilin phosphorylation, preventing actin filament repmodeling and neuronal degeneration, indicating the involvement of LIMK1 in Abeta- induced neuronal degeneration in vitro.", annotationSubgraph:["Regulation of actin cytoskeleton subgraph"], pmid:"16775141", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"16775141"}}] AS row
MATCH (start:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.start._id})
MATCH (end:`UNIQUE IMPORT LABEL`{`UNIQUE IMPORT ID`: row.end._id})
CREATE (start)-[r:ASSOCIATION]->(end) SET r += row.properties;
UNWIND [{start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:3662}, properties:{evidence:"ABeta¸ was proposed as a regulator of ion channel function [27] and as essential for neuronal health . ABeta¸ is secreted from neurons in response to synaptic activity and that ABeta¸, in turn, down regulates synaptic transmission [29]. This negative feedback loop could operate as a physiological homeostatic mechanism to limit levels of neuronal activity", annotationSubgraph:["Amyloidogenic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["Medium"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:3687}, properties:{evidence:"Molecular targets of ABeta¸ induced synaptic dysfunction: The search for a mechanism by which ABeta¸ impairs synaptic plasticity has led to the identification of the cell surface receptors and signaling pathways mediating ABeta¸-induced synaptoxicity. Cell surface interaction sites reported for ABeta¸ include receptor for Advanced Glycation End products (RAGE) and NMDAR [152, 209]. ABeta¸ has been variously reported to directly affect the activity of NMDAR, possibly by binding to nAChRs, or intracellular mitochondrial cyclophilin D (CypD), mitochondrial ABeta¸ alcohol dehydrogenase (ABAD) or certain protein kinases. Examination of the evidence for these multiple activities of ABeta¸ and their affinity constants may distinguish direct binding partners from downstream effectors.", annotationSubgraph:["NMDA receptor", "Glutamatergic subgraph"], annotationConfidence:["High"], pmid:"20847424", citationType:"PubMed", citationRef:"20847424"}}, {start: {_id:6}, end: {_id:1905}, properties:{evidence:"Molecular targets of ABeta¸ induced synaptic dysfunction: The search for a mechanism by which ABeta¸ impairs synaptic plasticity has led to the identification of the cell surface receptors and signaling pathways mediating ABeta¸-induced synaptoxicity. Cell surface interaction sites reported for ABeta¸ include receptor for Advanced Glycation End products (RAGE) and N